## Contents
## 1. Introduction
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are the advanced porous crystalline materials constructed by coordination bonding between divergent metal clusters (like vertices) and polydentate (multitopic) organic ligands in an infinite array [1]. In terms of their porous network nature, most MOF materials exhibit a superior crystalline architecture with systematically arranged periodic pores/channels with innumerable possibilities of spatial topology networks [2,3]. With the aid of the unique properties (e.g., porosity, very large surface to volume ratios, and tunable geometry), the versatile applicabilities of MOFs have been demonstrated and/or proven from a variety of applications such as: hydrogen production [4], electrocatalysis [5,6], environmental treatment [7,8], gas adsorption and storage [9–11], separation [12], biosensing [13], optoelectronic/ electromagnetic sensing [14,15], fuel cells [16,17], and biomedicine (e.g., drug delivery, and cancer therapy) [18–23]. In light of these meritful properties and associated utilities, the rational design of new crystalline MOFs has been conceptualized to impart tuned porosity and to expand active functional sites [6,24,25]. In particular, most efforts for the controlled synthesis of MOF materials have been centering on the use of transition metal clusters (e.g., elements in groups 4 to 12 in the periodic table) as building blocks in light of their abundance, low cost, and fixed coordination numbers $\mathrm{CN}\ =\ 4$ , 6, 8, or 12) [26,27]. Although research on MOF-based materials has been conducted at a rapid pace over the last decade (2010–2019, Fig. 1), the use of rare-earth (RE) elements (e.g., lanthanide (Ln) series) as active nodes and the engineering of crystalline RE-based MOFs (or LnMOFs) have yet been made to a limited degree. This is due to several limiting factors related to the RE-elements (e.g., a wide range of $\mathsf{C N}=2{-}12$ (because of an incomplete filled shell of 4f electrons), along with their high cost and toxicity concerns in the free ion forms (e.g., $\mathbf{G}\mathbf{d}^{3+})$ ) [28,29]. Nonetheless, research on the modular design of luminescent RE-MOFs for medical applications has been expanded noticeably in recent years (since 2012, Fig. 1). According to the literature data, the modular design of RE-MOFs geometry has been frequently undertaken by alternating (or substituting) ligands. However, there has not been much consideration on the direct control of the coordination numbers (or geometries) around the RE metal center in the course of their synthesis [30–32].
Fundamentally, the incorporation of RE metal cations as building blocks is an effective option to create specific luminescent REMOFs with unique electronic, optical/luminescent, and magnetic properties [33–35]. This should reflect the shielded nature of $\bar{5}s^{2}\bar{5}\mathsf{p}^{6}$ filled orbitals around the 4f electrons of the RE atoms. Accordingly, the prominent electronic/magnetic features of REelements are unlikely to be altered much by the conditions of the external environments (e.g., the coordinating ligands) [36]. In particular, these features of RE-MOFs have been articulated to offer definite advantages in biomedical (e.g., bio-imaging and drug delivery) and sensing fields [21]. Likewise, their promising roles are recognized for the controlled delivery/release of many drug molecules [20,33,37,38]. It should however be noted that REMOFs should preferably be scaled down to the submicron or nanoscale regime for such applications. In the biomedical field, the use of RE-MOFs nanocarriers with a large external surface area has been proven to be an effective option to regulate their in vivo fate (e.g., in terms of circulation and targeting properties). Hence, tuning of the RE-MOF surface area can be a promising option to control their toxicity level and/or bio-activity in biomedical applications [39]. Specifically, the utility of nanoscale RE-MOFs is recognized in the renal/splenic clearance system (e.g., clearance routes: $>200\ \mathrm{nm}$ via splenic filtration and $<10\mathrm{nm}$ via kidney filtering system) [39,40]. As such, the engineering of nanoscale RE-MOFs has become a highly demanding subject in numerous biomedical applications [41–43].
Indeed, it is challenging to design luminescent RE-MOFs with high quantum yields via simple in situ synthesis strategies (e.g., solvothermal methods) because of the high coordination number of RE (or $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ ) metal ions and their flexible coordination geometry [44,45]. In addition, the triplet excited state metal-centered luminescence of $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ can be quenched after coordination with the oxygen or nitrogen groups of solvent molecules. As such, these quenching groups can lead to significant attenuation in the quantum yield of RE-MOFs [46]. The number of researches focusing on the modulated synthesis of RE-MOFs has been increasing continuously to help uplift their luminescence/quantum yield. In this regard, the structural diversity of RE-MOFs (e.g., topological structures) has recently been reviewed in relation to the nature of the RE-coordination chemistry (e.g., RE-metal ions, RE-metal clusters, and RE-oxo/hydroxo chain nodes) [36]. It was reported that the RE-ions play an integral role in regulating the RE-MOF structure. Apart from this, the geometries of RE-MOFs are controlled by the coordinated inorganic structural units $(\mathsf{R E}_{(\mathrm{III})}.$ -cluster vs. $\mathsf{R E}_{(\mathrm{III})^{-}}$ chain nodes) and ligand steric effects. The selection of RE-metal nodes is hence recognized to be crucial for tuning the topological properties of RE-MOFs. However, a variety of in situ synthesis methods available for the nanoscale RE-MOFs are not yet clearly defined in relation to the principles of their designing [36]. Further, relatively little is known about the effect of electronic properties of RE(III) ions on the unique characteristics between different REMOFs (e.g., in terms of luminescent, electronic, and magnetic features). Also, there are still ongoing challenges to upgrade the potential of RE-MOFs for their targeted applications along with their controllable synthesis.
In this review, an up-to-date critical evaluation on the eminent features of RE-MOFs was made in relation to their optical, luminescent, electronic, and magnetic resonance properties. Our aim was also extended to provide a comprehensive overview of the controlled synthesis and post-modification strategies for a number of important nanoscale RE-MOFs. A detailed discussion was made on the current status of RE-MOF research, along with the role of their constituents (RE-metal nodes and the nature of ligands), in biomedical and biosensing applications with the aid of the assessment and evaluation of their performance. It is believed that the information discussed in this work could help transfer the explicit knowledge needed for further expansion of versatile applicabilities of RE-MOFs.
## 2. Eminent characteristics of RE-MOFs
According to the IUPAC definition, RE elements mainly consist of 15 lanthanides in the periodic table [from lanthanum (La) to lutetium (Lu)] along with yttrium (Y) and scandium (Sc) [47]. The concentration of these RE elements in their main ores rarely exceeds $10\mathrm{-}15\%$ , whereas their abundance in the Earth’s crust is in the range of 0.5 (e.g., Tm and Lu) to $60~\mathrm{ppm}$ (e.g., Y) [46]. Note that almost all RE elements are highly stable in a trivalent oxidation state $(\mathrm{Ln}^{+3})$ , although there are some RE elements which are also stable in other oxidation states (e.g., $\mathrm{Eu}^{+2}$ , $\mathsf{C e}^{+4}$ , $S\mathrm{m}^{+2}$ , $\mathsf{P r}^{+4}$ , $\mathsf{Y b}^{+2}$ , and $\mathrm{{Tb}^{+4}}$ ) [46]. As lanthanides mainly exist in the trivalent state $\{\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}\colon[\mathrm{Xe}]4\mathrm{f}^{\mathrm{n}}(0<\mathrm{n}<14)$ ; with a spin of $+\%$ or $-\%$ , they maintain high photostability and well-defined $4\mathrm{f}\rightarrow4\mathrm{f}$ transitions with sharp luminescence spectra (UV–visible to NIR (nearinfrared) regions) [48]. For example, in the UV region, the ${\mathsf{C e}}^{3+}$ ion exhibits a broadband UV emission $5{\mathsf{d}}\to4{\mathsf{f}}$ transition) in the range of 370 to $410\ \mathrm{nm}$ [49]. In the visible region, ${\sf E u}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ , $\mathsf{S}\mathsf{m}^{3+}$ , and $\mathrm{Tm}^{3+}$ ions emit red, green, orange, and blue light, respectively. In addition, ${\mathsf{N}}{\mathsf{d}}^{3+}$ , $\Upsilon\mathsf{b}^{3+}$ , $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Pr}}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Er}^{3+}$ , and ${\mathrm{Ho}}^{3+}$ ions display distinct luminescence in the NIR region [50]. As such, RE (or Ln) elements are frequently utilized for the synthesis of fluorescent/ phosphorescent RE-MOFs [51,52].
Due to Coulombic and spin–orbit interactions between f electrons, the electronic configuration states of $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ ions can be split into several spectroscopic levels, marked as $^{(2S+1)}\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{J}}$ with $(2{\mathrm{~J~+~}}1)$ multiplicity according to the Russell–Saunders (RS) notation [53]. Here, L, S, and J denote the orbital, electron spin, and total angular momenta (i.e., ${\boldsymbol{\mathrm{J}}}={\boldsymbol{\mathrm{L}}}+{\boldsymbol{\mathrm{S}}} $ vectors), respectively, in units of ⁄. Indeed, there are several ways to associate the $n$ electrons with the seven wavefunctions of 4f orbitals with consideration of the spin-orbital values. In general, the ground level of RE elements can be computed using Hund’s rule 3 $(\mathrm{J}=\mathrm{J}_{\mathrm{min}}$ if $\begin{array}{r}{n<(2\ell+1)}\end{array}$ and ${\mathrm{J}}={\mathrm{J}}_{\mathrm{max}}{\mathrm{if}}\ n>$ $(2\ell+1),$ [46]. On this basis, the J levels referred to as a ‘‘multiplet” are equal electron spin (S) levels $(\mathrm{J}=\mathsf{S}$ at $\mathrm{L}=0$ ) if the subshell is half-filled with electrons $(\boldsymbol{\mathrm{n}}\boldsymbol{=}~(2\ell~+1)~=~7^{\cdot}$ . As such, the J levels may take a half-integer value when the set of S is a half-integer. Additionally, the energy of the spin-orbital levels can increase with increasing value of the multiplet level (J), if $\mathsf{n}<(2\ell+1=7)$ . For instance, the $\mathsf{E u}^{3+}\ (4\mathsf{f}^{6})$ ground term is split into seven spectroscopic levels (i.e., $^7\mathrm{F}_{0},\phantom{^7}\mathrm{F}_{1},\phantom{^7}\mathrm{F}_{2},\phantom{^7}\mathrm{F}_{3},\phantom{^7}\mathrm{F}_{4},\phantom{^7}\mathrm{F}_{5}$ , and $^7\mathrm{F}_{6}\mathrm{.}$ , where $^7\mathrm{F}_{0}$ is the ground level with multiplicities of 49. The energy difference $(\Delta\mathbb{E})$ between two consecutive spin-orbital levels is directly proportional to J’ $\langle\Delta\mathrm{E}=\lambda\mathrm{J}^{\prime}$ , where $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{J}^{\prime}=\mathrm{J}+1}$ ) [46]. Table 1 summarizes the electronic properties of some of the selected trivalent lanthanides (4f) ions.
Each of the lanthanide ions $(\mathrm{Ln}^{3+})$ exhibits different electronic energy levels due to the electronic configuration of $\mathsf{4f^{n}}$ orbitals $(\mathrm{n}=0{-}14)$ . The energy levels of these trivalent ions tend to be insensitive to the surrounding chemicals, if existing, because of the shielded nature of $5s^{2}5\mathsf{p}^{6}$ filled orbitals and the larger radial expansion of the xenon [Xe] core [50]. Therefore, the 4f electrons of most $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ ions are not involved in bonding during the formation of coordination complexes. In fact, they behave similarly to the free ions [48]. Such behavior allows most $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ ions (except for ${\mathsf{C e}}^{3+}$ , ${\mathsf{L}}{\mathsf{a}}^{3+}$ , and ${\mathrm{Lu}}^{3+}$ ) to maintain their luminescent property originated from the f-f transitions. The luminescence of $\mathsf{C e}^{3+}$ would depend upon the rearrangement of electrons in the f-orbital subshells (from 5d to 4f transitions: i.e., the coordination environment of ${5}\mathrm{d}^{1}$ excited state). In contrast, both ${\mathsf{L}}{\mathsf{a}}^{3+}$ and ${\mathrm{Lu}}^{3+}$ ions are inert to luminescent emission due to the absence of electrons $\left(\mathsf{L}\mathsf{a}^{3+}\colon\mathsf{f}^{0}\right)$ or the complete filling of electrons in the f-orbital $(\mathrm{Lu}^{3+}\colon\mathrm{f}^{14})$ .
According to quantum mechanics, most $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ ions yield very low molar extinction coefficients (or molar absorptivity) $\mathrm{\Phi}(\mathrm{\varepsilon}<10\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ . $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ ) upon their direct excitation. This is because of the restriction on the interconfigurational f-f-transitions [21]. This limitation can be overcome if $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ ions are incorporated into the lattice of crystalline MOFs materials (Fig. 2). Accordingly, the RE-MOFs (or Ln-MOFs) can lead to improvement of the quantum yields/ luminescence features of RE centers via an indirect luminescence sensitization process caused by the ‘‘antenna effect” of the chromophoric ligand within the framework [6,54]. In this respect, the mechanism of antenna sensitization within the RE-MOFs can be accounted for by three steps [21,51].
(1) The organic ligand surrounding $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ ions acts as a photonharvesting system (excited-state absorption (ESA)). (2) The excited ligand (ESA) then transfers the photon energy to the $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ ion (for excitation) by an intramolecular energy transfer (ligand-to-metal) mechanism. (3) The excitation of $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ ions generate strong luminescent spectra with a high quantum yield $(\Phi)$ .
Such transitions provide RE-MOFs with excellent optical absorption and emission properties with characteristics of large Stokes shifts, sharp emission profiles, and long luminescence lifetimes that are resistant to blinking and photobleaching [28,48,55,56]. Accordingly, these unique photoluminescent properties of the RE-MOFs (or Ln-MOFs) make them excellent candidates for sensing and imaging/sensing applications in biomedicaloriented fields.
## 2.1. Electronic/luminescence properties
The electronic configurations of the ground $\{[\mathsf{X e}]4\mathsf{f}^{\mathrm{n}};\mathrm{n}=0-14$ } and excited states $[\mathrm{Xe}]4\mathrm{f}^{\mathrm{n}-1}5\mathrm{d}^{1}$ $^{\prime}\Delta\mathrm{E}>32,000{\mathrm{cm}}^{-1}.$ of $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ ions are separated from each other [46]. The energy required for the excitation of lanthanides is dependent on the number of electrons in the f-orbital (n). Specifically, the coordination number ( $\mathsf{C N}=8$ or 9) of $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ ions is influenced by their ionic radii, which decreases with increasing number of electrons in the f-orbital (i.e., from ${\mathsf{L}}{\mathsf{a}}^{3+}$ $\displaystyle([\mathsf{X e}]\mathsf{f}^{0})$ to $\mathrm{Lu}^{3+}(\mathrm{[Xe]f}^{14});$ . The lanthanides with larger ionic radii have a coordination number of nine, while those with smaller radii have a coordination number of eight [48]. As such, the energy of $4\mathrm{f}^{\mathrm{n}-1}5\mathrm{d}^{1}$ in the RE-MOF lattices decreases with the increasing number of electrons in the f-orbital from 1 to 14. This is likely accounted for by the reduced interaction of the filled 4f subshell $\mathrm{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{{4}d^{10}}$ -electrons) with the surrounding coordinated organic ligand/- solvent molecules [57].
In terms of optical properties, the interconfigurational $4\mathrm{f}^{\mathrm{n-1}}5\mathrm{d}^{1}\leftrightarrow4\mathrm{f}^{\mathrm{n}}$ transitions are mainly governed by the spin selection rule based on the type of lanthanide. In contrast, the rates of forbidden $4\mathsf{f}^{\mathrm{n}}\leftrightarrow4\mathsf{f}^{\mathrm{n}}$ transitions depend on the local symmetry of the luminescence center in the lattice structure [57]. In general, all trivalent $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ ions (e.g., ${\mathsf{C e}}^{3+}$ and $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Pr}}^{3+}$ : $4\mathrm{f}^{\mathrm{n-1}}5\mathrm{d}^{1}\rightarrow4\mathrm{f}^{\mathrm{n}}$ transitions) exhibit high energy luminescence in the NIR region, making them useful for applications in lasers and scintillators [58,59]. In contrast, the divalent $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Ln}^{2+}$ ions (e.g., $4\mathrm{f}^{6}5\mathrm{d}^{1}\ \rightarrow\ 4\mathrm{f}^{7}(\mathrm{Y}\mathsf{b}^{2+})$ and $4\mathrm{f}^{13}5\mathrm{d}^{1}\rightarrow4\mathrm{f}^{14}(\mathrm{Eu}^{2+}))$ displayed luminescence energy in the visible region, which is suitable for optoelectronic sensing applications [60,61]. Table 2 shows the number of electronic sub-levels of the ligand-field vs. the J value (when it is an integer or half-integer) for the determination of the symmetry point group from f–f emission/absorption spectra in their coordination frameworks.
Based on Laporte’s rule (spin-parity forbidden for $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ ion electronic (f-f) transitions), the Stokes shifts and emission intensity of $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ ions can be improved significantly by the synthesis of RE-MOF materials. Table 3 shows the typical luminescent emission for $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ ion spectra along with other photophysical parameters. Specifically, in Fig. 2a, the luminescence property of RE-MOFs can be attributed to the emission phenomena of (i) RE metal nodes (charge transfer via $4\mathrm{f}\rightarrow5\mathrm{d}$ transitions), (ii) chromophore ligands (antennae effect), and (iii) the formation of exciplexes/excimers within the framework [49,51].
In most luminescent RE-MOFs, $\pi$ -rich conjugated ligands (with molar absorptivity $\mathrm{(\varepsilon)}>10^{5}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}.\mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ are reported to be a crucial component for enhancement of the emission property. Within the coordination framework, the ligands act as antennas (photon energy absorbers) for RE metal nodes. The major migration path for exciton energy is expected to comply with intersystemcrossing for both the short-lived singlet excited state $(\mathsf{S}_{1})$ and the long-lived triplet state $\left(\mathrm{T}_{1}\right)$ of the ligand (Fig. 2b) [48]. The subsequent radiative transition of absorbed photon energy from antennas $\left(\mathrm{T}_{1}\right)$ to the RE (or Ln) emitting states (via ligand–metal charge transfer (LMCT) mechanism) leads to the induction of sharp and narrow luminescent emission of RE-MOFs [62]. This phenomenon is known as sensitization (or antenna effect), which involves light harvesting, the LMCT mechanism, and light emission processes. In some cases, metal–ligand and inter-ligand charge transfer (MLCT and ILCT: Fig. 2c) processes have also been reported to play a part in ligand-based emission in luminescent RE-MOFs for sensing applications [51]. In this respect, the excited state of a ligand upon light irradiation can form short-lived homo-/heterodimeric complexes. These short-lived homo- and heterodimeric complexes are defined as excimers and exciplexes, respectively [55]. The excimer and exciplex complexes in RE-MOFs can be formed via $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions between the excited states of two adjacent conjugated ligands and/or between the excited state of ligands and encapsulated guest molecules upon photon absorption. The formation of excimers or exciplexes has also been found to be dependent on the orientation of the RE-MOF components or functional groups [55]. As such, the lowest triplet state of the sensitizing ligand plays a vital role in the entire sensitization process due to its effect on the energy transfer mechanisms, as shown in Fig. 2 $({\mathsf{b}}{-}{\mathsf{c}})$ [46].
Overall, the energy transfer processes in RE-MOFs can be explained by theoretical mechanisms based on dipole–dipole (Förster) and double-electron exchanges (Dexter) [63]. The efficiency of the former route maintains an inverse proportionality to the sixth power of the length of the coordination bond in the RE-MOF frameworks because the energy transfer mechanism takes place through the space between the ligands and $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ metal sites (up to $10\ \mathrm{nm}$ ). Unlike the Förster mechanism, the Dexter energy transfer rate decays as the bond distance between the two building units of RE-MOFs increases due to the hopping of electrons (i.e., exciton transfer from a donor to acceptor). However, it should be noted that if their distance is too short or too long, coordination bonds can be subjected to luminescent quenching or poor energy transfer processes [48]. In addition, the Dexter mechanism is commonly coupled with quenching (i.e., the decay of molecular fluorescence). The RE-MOFs luminescence can be quenched significantly by the presence of high-energy vibrators (e.g., O–H, C–H, and ${\mathsf{N}}{\mathsf{-}}{\mathsf{H}}{\overbar{\mathbf{\Gamma}}}$ ) from coordinating solvent molecules (such as MeOH, DMF, and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ ) near RE (or Ln) metal centers [32,49,64]. This phenomenon is known as non-radiative multiphonon relaxation of luminescence emission, which is accompanied by a decrease of the quantum yields of RE-MOFs and emission lifetimes, particularly in the NIR region. Therefore, future research should be directed toward the minimization of these quenching groups (e.g., optimizing luminescence RE-MOFs design structure) by appropriate selection of functional ligands and/or encapsulated guest molecules [41,54].
## 2.2. Magnetic resonance features
To date, RE-MOFs have attracted great attention for application as contrast agents (CA) in multimode imaging (e.g., magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) and optical imaging) [65–67]. As a general rule, the preference of RE-MOFs as CAs materials in MRI bioimaging can be attributed to the up-conversion luminescent (Xrays absorption efficiency) and paramagnetic features of trivalent rare earth elements [28]. With the aid of enhanced magnetic fields, the spatial resolution of MRI can be ameliorated with a high $S/\mathsf{N}$ ratio to efficiently delineate the regions of interest with a shorter imaging acquisition time [44]. Basically, the relaxivity $(r_{i})$ term is used to express the effectiveness of RE-MOFs as CAs in MRI imaging. A higher $r_{i}$ value of RE-MOF corresponds to a more effective contrast imaging capability [68].
MRI spectroscopy is a widely used noninvasive clinical diagnosis tool. Its utility includes the anatomical diagnosis of internal organs/soft tissues, chemotherapy response assessment, and tumor detection [44,69]. MRI relies on the analysis of nuclear spin reorientations of water protons in human tissues when such tissues are exposed to a radiofrequency pulse under a magnetic field [44,70]. The signal intensity of MRI and associated image quality depend on the relaxation time [the longitudinal $\left(\mathsf{T}_{1}\right)$ and the transverse $\left(\mathrm{T}_{2}\right)]$ of proton spins to its equilibrium state [71]. In general, rapid ${{\mathrm{T}}_{1}}$ -weighted relaxation leads to the generation of brighter
MRI images with higher net electromagnetic signals. On the other hand, faster $\mathrm{T}_{2}$ -weighted relaxation decreases signal intensity due to the rapid dephasing of individual spins [70]. Notably, the sensitivity of MRI for delineating tumors and/or internal organs is relatively low. This is due to the low response signal of proton magnetic moments (the difference between normal and abnormal organs/tissues) relative to noise (signal-to-noise $(S/\mathsf{N})$ ratio) [44]. As such, the use of contrast agents (CAs) is frequently administered to improve the 3D tomographic imaging quality during MRI scans via shortening the relaxation time (i.e., relaxivity $(r_{i)}\mathrm{~:~}$ where $i=1$ (for ${{\mathrm{T}}_{1}}$ ) or 2 (for ${\mathrm{T}}_{2}{\mathrm{\cdot}}$ )) of the proton spins) [72,73]. Detailed information on the classical theories and derived models for proton spin relaxation during MR contrast imaging can be found elsewhere [74,75]. In fact, a range of Ln-chelate complexes (e.g., $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Ln}}=\ensuremath{\mathrm{G}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{d}}^{3+}$ , $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ , and $\mathsf{M n}^{2+}$ ) are available for clinical MRI contrast imaging, such as Gadovist, OptiMARK, Dotarem, Feridex, ProHance, Omis$\mathsf{c a n}^{\mathbb{\infty}}$ , Magnevist, MultiHance, and Primovist [76].
Among RE elements, $\mathsf{G d}^{3+}$ -based complexes are utilized commercially as CAs due to the high paramagnetism and long electronic relaxation time of the $\mathsf{G d}^{3+}$ ion (radius $=108~\mathrm{pm}$ ) [77–79]. $\mathsf{G d}^{3+}$ -based chelate complexes (Fig. 3), GBCAs materials, have been used most widely for MRI scan analysis [75,76,80]. Basically, almost all $\mathsf{G d}^{3+}$ -based CAs (GBCAs) are known as positive CAs because of the large number of $\mathsf{G d}^{3+}$ ion unpaired electrons $(S=7/2)$ . Positive GBCAs lead to improved MRI signal intensity with bright MRI images via shortening ${{\mathrm{T}}_{1}}$ along with an increase of the relaxivity ratio $(r_{2}/r_{1})$ to 1 [44]. Recently, however, some clinical reports had demonstrated an accumulation of $\mathsf{G d}^{3+}$ ions in patient tissues (e.g., skin, bone, brain, liver, and kidneys) when GBCAs were administered during MRI measurements [81–83]. This issue is believed to be caused by the lower stability of various types of GBCAs (e.g., dissociation of the $\mathsf{G d}^{3+}$ ion coordination complexes) [84]. Additionally, it has been reported that the leached free $\mathsf{G d}^{3+}$ ions from GBCAs can inhibit some physiological processes in human cells (e.g., enzymes activity and voltage-gated calcium channels) as they start competing with free ${\mathsf{C a}}^{2+}$ ions $\cdot114\mathrm{pm})$ in the body. These toxic effects of free $\mathsf{G d}^{3+}$ ions in human cells limit the utilization of traditional GBCAs in MRI diagnosis [44,85,86].
To avoid the toxicity of traditional GBCAs, the use of numerous RE-MOFs as alternative candidates has recently been recommended to offer a new class of multimodal CAs $(\mathsf{T}_{1}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{T}_{2}$ dualmode CAs) [71,87]. In multimodal imaging, the RE-MOFs can simultaneously tune the $\mathrm{T}_{1}\mathrm{\cdot}$ - and $\mathrm{T}_{2}$ -relaxation rates of proton spin to improve bioimage and diagnostic sensitivity [20,28,50]. A SciFinder/Google scholar search made using ‘‘lanthanide MOFs in MRI as contrast agents” returned 1,340 articles from 2006 to 2020. Over this period, there has been a noticeable growth in the number of publications involving RE-MOFs (Ln-MOFs) with respect to their synthesis and application toward MRI contrast imaging. Such a promising trend is likely to reflect their unique advantages such as luminescent (UV–visible/NIR) properties, long-circulating times, good stability, low cytotoxicity, and ease of functionalization to produce a modulation design [28,54,88]. However, it is vital to note that the relaxivity rate (Fig. 4) of RE-MOFs as contrast imaging materials in various in vivo studies can be influenced by some key variables such as the binding interaction of RE-MOFs with biological tissues and/or the protons flowing in a blood vessel. In addition, the contrast imaging of RE-MOFs (both in vitro and in vivo) can also be affected by an interplay between the RE-MOF properties (e.g., particle size, magnetic strength, and stability) and reaction variables (e.g., temperature, pH, CA concentrations, and type of medium) [69]. For instance, the relaxivity rate can be so high that no signal is technically detectable if the RE-MOFs have an intense magnetic moment or their concentrations are very high.
In other words, the upper limit for the amount of RE-MOF-based CAs should be considered during an MRI scan along with the toxicity effects of RE metal nodes.
The feasibility of RE-MOFs for NIR contrast imaging in living cells (e.g., HeLa human cancer and NIH 3 T3 mouse cells) was first reported by using nanoscale a Yb-PVDC-3 (PVDC ligand: phenylenevinylene-dicarboxylate) MOF [89]. The nanoscale YbPVDC-3 MOF $\langle316\pm156\ \mathrm{nm}$ length, $176\pm52~\mathrm{{nm}}$ width) exhibits a low symmetry space group $P{-}1$ with 1D channels and $a$ - crystallographic direction $(\mathrm{Y}\mathsf{b}^{3+}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{Y}\mathsf{b}^{3+}$ center-to-center distances: $43\times9\mathring{\mathsf{A}},$ ). Upon excitation at $450\ \mathrm{nm}$ in $0.1\mathrm{~M~}$ HEPES buffer or water $\mathsf{\Pi}_{\mathrm{[pH}~7.3)}$ , the Yb-PVDC-3 MOF showed NIR-luminescence emission (e.g., at ${970}\mathrm{nm}$ ) from the indirect sensitization by a PVDC ligand (quantum yield $(\Phi)$ of $5.2\pm{0.8}\times10^{-5}$ and $1.0\pm{0.3}\times{10^{-4}}$ in buffer and water, respectively). Notably, the obtained Yb-PVDC3 showed high stability (up to 4 weeks) and low toxicity $\mathrm{LC}_{50}$ of $100~\upmu\mathrm{g/mL})$ in cellular media along with good sensitivity with a 1 s exposure time to speed up the NIR microscopy experiments.
These properties make Yb-PVDC-3 a potential NIR-emitting media for live-cell imaging. Although free $\mathsf{G d}^{3+}$ ions have been reported to be toxic for biological cells, Gd-based coordination polymers (CPs) and RE-MOFs are predominantly being reported for MRI contrast imaging (Table 4). This is likely because of the slow electronic relaxation rate and high electronic spin value $(S=7/2)$ of $\mathsf{G d}^{3+}$ ions as a metal node [20]. In addition, the tuned surface chemistry of RE-MOFs, along with their high surface-to-volume $(S/\mathsf{V})$ ratio, is expected to play a vital role in enhancing contrast imaging [28,44,70].
In Table 4, it can also be noted that the relaxivity $(r_{1})$ values seem to follow an increased trend order with decreasing dimensional size of the RE-MOF particles. A decrease of the dimension (surface area) of nanoscale RE-MOFs permits a higher number of $\mathsf{G d}^{3+}$ ions to be available for interaction/coordination with water protons at their surface [79]. As such, the high $S/\mathsf{V}$ ratio, along with the porous structure of RE-MOFs, can promote their coordination bonding with water molecules [69]. Such a phenomenon can favorably accelerate electron transition/exchange between the $\mathsf{G d}^{3+}$ node and bulk water in the second coordination shell, thereby increasing the relaxivity rate of the proton spins to the ground state [68]. These overall observations suggest that the enhanced imaging capabilities of RE-MOFs are closely tied to their large surface area, which imparts the best relaxation properties.
