A new nanoscale RE-MOF $\mathrm{50}\pm12\mathrm{{nm}}$ ) containing a bimetallic node $\mathrm{\Delta}G\mathsf{d}^{3+}$ and ${\mathsf{E u}}^{3+}$ ) and silica coating $(\mathrm{Eu},\mathrm{Gd-MOF@SiO_{2}N P S})$ has been developed to enhance contrast imaging in $\mathrm{T}_{1}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{T}_{2}$ dual-mode MRI [71]. This RE-MOF was prepared by hydrothermal coordination of $\mathsf{G d}^{3+}$ and ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ ions with isophthalic acid $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{IP}\mathrm{A}\right)$ in the presence of polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP) and hexamethylenetetramine (HMTA) in DMF/water mixed solvent at $100^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $4\mathrm{min}$ , followed by post-synthesis modification of a $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ coating (inset TEM image in Fig. 6a). Remarkably, the developed $\mathsf{E u},\mathsf{G d-M O F@S i O_{2}}$ NPs showed strong emission at 620 and $594~\mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. 6a) caused by ${}^{5}\mathrm{D}_{0}\rightarrow{}^{7}\mathrm{F}_{2}$ and $\ensuremath{{}^{5}\mathrm{D}}_{0}\ensuremath{\rightarrow{}^{7}\mathrm{F}_{1}}$ transitions, respectively. In terms of MRI imaging, the Eu,G $\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{d-M O F@S i O_{2}}}$ NPs exhibited extraordinarily dual-mode relaxivities $(r_{1}/r_{2}$ of $38/222\ \mathrm{\mM}^{-1}\ \mathrm{\s}^{-1})$ , higher than those of commonly employed clinical CAs, e.g., $r_{1}$ of 4.95 (GdDTPA) and $r_{2}$ of $117\:\mathrm{\mM}^{-1}\:\mathrm{\s}^{-1}$ (Feridex). In addition, Eu,Gd$\mathsf{M O F@S i O_{2}}$ NPs showed good biocompatibility and low cytotoxicity for the U87MG human glioblastoma cell line, even at high concentrations $50~\upmu\mathrm{M}~\mathsf{G}\mathsf{d}^{3+})$ , along with high stability over a broad pH (4–9) range (Fig. 6b-c). By using in vivo MRI (7 T magnet), the injection of $\mathsf{E u},\mathsf{G d-M O F@S i O_{2}}$ NPs into U87MG tumor cells and $\mathsf{B A L B}/\mathsf{c}$ mice cells $(0.8~\mathrm{mg/kg)}$ was seen to significantly improve signals in ${{\mathrm{T}}_{1}}$ -weighted images while reducing the signal in $\mathrm{T}_{2}$ -weighted images. In detail, after $^\mathrm{~1~h~}$ of injection into U87MG tumor cells, the average MRI signals improved by $12\pm6\%$ in Ti-weighted images while declining by $89\pm2\%$ in $\mathrm{T}_{2}$ -weighted images. In $\mathsf{B A L B}/\mathrm{c}$ mice cells, the signals of ${\sf T}_{1}$ -weighted images for the liver increased remarkably to $157\pm9\%$ after $^{1\mathrm{h}}$ of injection but declined to $105\pm2\%$ after $^{4\mathrm{~h~}}$ of injection, relative to the pre-MRI scans (Fig. 6d). On the other hand, the $\mathrm{T}_{2}$ -weighted image signal was found to decrease from $57\pm12\%$ to $38\pm16\%1$ and $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ after injection into $\mathsf{B A L B}/\mathsf{c}$ mice cells, respectively. This is likely to reflect the effect of signal saturation on the improved MRI signal caused by considerable bioaccumulation of RE-MOF NPs in the tumor cells during the MRI measurements.
In summary, in comparison to traditional GBCA, RE-MOFs can be regarded as potential multifunctional candidates for multimodal $\mathrm{T}_{1}\mathrm{-}\big/\mathrm{T}_{2}$ -weighted MRI contrast imaging due to their high relaxivities for proton spins during MRI scans. Nonetheless, additional efforts should be directed to reduce the in vivo toxicity risks of RE-MOFs by increasing their stability in human cells, particularly for Gd-based MOFs. These two vital issues need to be addressed in the future to make RE-MOFs viable for MRI contrast agents.
## 3. Synthesis routes of RE-MOF crystals
Controlled production of RE-MOF crystals can provide promise for many potential applications of these new functional platforms with specific morphology and up-conversion luminescence features. Properly tuned characteristics of RE-MOFs allow their enhanced performances in biomedical applications such as molecular targeting, cellular sensing/imaging, and drug delivery [44,91]. Based on the energy source and reaction mechanisms, the synthetic routes of RE-MOF crystals can be grouped into two main classes: solvothermal and non-solvothermal methods. As examined comprehensively in some earlier studies [92,93], luminescence properties and the charge transfer mechanisms in RE-MOFs can be attributed to a number of factors: (1) organic ligands luminescence properties, (2) rare-earth metal centers (referable to ‘‘antenna effect”); and (3) guest-induced luminescence introduced into MOFs. Accordingly, the RE-MOFs can be classified into three groups based on the synthetic approach and luminescent mechanism: (i) direct luminescent RE-MOF crystals, (ii) 2-D luminescent RE-MOF films, and (iii) host–guest luminescent RE-MOF composites [92]. In terms of host–guest luminescent RE-MOFs, the PSF of pristine MOFs with RE ions has commonly been proposed as an effective route to design a new class of luminescent RE-MOF hybrid materials (See section 3.4) with great potential in fluorescence sensing applications [94,95].
Notably, the growth of RE-MOFs crystals is mainly governed by an interplay between the metal salt/organic linker type and the reagent concentrations, which further exert the control on the rate of nucleation and coordination conditions during their synthesis. The detailed operation parameters for the tuning or engineering of luminescent RE-MOFs nanoparticle have also been reviewed by many research groups [2,45,92,93,96]. Table 5 also summarizes the advantages and disadvantages of synthesis approaches employed to control the orientation and functionalization of REMOF crystals.
## 3.1. Solvo(hydro)thermal synthesis methods
Solvothermal (or hydrothermal) is the most widely used conventional procedure to develop microscale RE-MOF crystals by direct coordination between blended building blocks (lanthanide ions and organic ligands) under high temperatures (e.g., above the boiling point of the solvent, for example, $100{-}280~^{\circ}C$ [97]). In this self-assembly method, the rate of nucleation and growth of RE-MOF crystals is directly dependent on the applied temperature, which is the main route to enforce the coordination between RE ions and organic ligand molecules in the presence or absence of generated pressure (e.g., solvent vapor in a closed reactor) [3]. In general, solvo(hydro)thermal synthesis of RE-MOFs can be carried out by using a traditional electrical oven or microwave irradiation as energy sources. Fundamentally, the nucleation rate of RE-MOFs is usually promoted by increasing the operating temperature (or microwave power) when using a low boiling point polar solvent [98,99]. Such a condition can favorably accelerate solvent evaporation to raise the partial pressure in a closed reactor. Accordingly, the combined effect of high partial pressure and temperature can simultaneously contribute to the fast nucleation and growth of RE-MOF crystals.
Traditional solvo(hydro)thermal is frequently used to generate single-phase RE-MOF crystals with controlled shape properties (size and morphology) via a slow crystal nucleation rate under low-temperature conditions over an extended reaction period (e.g., week–month). Template-assisted strategies (by using structure-directing agents (SDAs)) have also been adopted, which permit selective control of the orientation of RE-MOF crystals [93,99]. However, the use of traditional solvothermal is an energy and time-consuming procedure (hours to days).
To overcome the limitations described above, microwave irradiation can be employed to increase the rate of kinetic energy for the acceleration of the nucleation/growth process of RE-MOF crystals [100]. In this microwave-assisted method, electromagnetic waves are transformed into heat energy via direct interaction with mobile electric charges in the reaction media (e.g., polar solvent molecules, organic linkers, and Ln ions) to achieve high reaction temperatures [101]. As such, the accurate growth of RE-MOFs with a well-defined geometry and tunable size can be manipulated by controlling some key parameters (e.g., coordination modulators) such as the energy power (microwave irradiation and heating temperature rate), reaction time, solvent nature (pH and polarity), and concentration of reactants [3,102]. Slight alterations of these modulator variables may generate varying coordination numbers between the RE ions and ligand during RE-MOF crystal growth and thereby influence the framework sizes and morphologies. Therefore, it is important to determine the optimum solvo(hydrothermal) conditions, which accelerate the synthesis processes and produce tunable RE-MOFs with the required luminescent properties.
It should be noted that the solvent or SDA template molecules can be easily coordinated with the metal site or trapped in the micropores of RE-MOFs during solvo(hydro)thermal synthesis. These trapped impurities can reduce the luminescence and performance of RE-MOFs in medical and sensing applications. For example, a variety of nanomorphic $\mathrm{Eu(BTC)(H_{2}O)}$ DMF $\mathrm{(H_{3}B T C=}$ 1,3,5-Benzenetricarboxylic acid; DMF $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ N,N-dimethylformamide) specimens were prepared by adjusting the solvothermal reaction temperature and solvent solution in the presence of sodium acetate additive as an SDA agent [103]. Accordingly, a change of the DMF $/\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ solvent mixture ratio is essential in the regulation of the MOF crystal size (Fig. 7). However, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ can act not only as a solvent to adjust the solubility of metal salts during nucleation and crystal growth but also as a reactant for coordination with metal centers. Similarly, sodium acetate was utilized as an SDA agent $(8~\mathrm{mmol})$ ) to control the growth rate of Ln-MOFs crystals. It competed with the carboxyl group of the $\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B T C}$ ligand during coordination with $\operatorname{Ln}(\operatorname{III})$ metal ions $\langle\mathrm{Ln}=\mathrm{Eu}$ , Tb, Sm, Dy, Nd, or Yb) [104]. Therefore, it is crucial to apply frequent washing with volatile solvents (such as ethanol or methanol) followed by vacuum drying (as a preferable option) to remove the trapped impurities (within pores) and axial-coordinated solvent molecules from the RE-MOF crystals, without collapse of the framework structures.
## 3.2. Non-solvothermal (or straightforward) synthesis methods
Non-solvothermal (or straightforward) eco-friendly synthesis strategies (e.g., mechanical, ultrasound, emulsion, nanoprecipitation, and electrochemical) are useful to confine the fast growth of nanoscale RE-MOFs [31,45,92]. The development of such a straightforward approach should also be energy efficient as the synthesis can be achieved at relatively low temperatures (near ambient or below the solvent’s boiling point) and atmospheric pressure (e.g., in an open chemical reactor) conditions.
The ultrasonic (ULS)-assisted synthesis relies on hydrodynamic phenomena called acoustic waves, which cause a substantial increase in the reaction temperature to ${\sim}5,000~\mathrm{K}$ and pressure to \~1,000 atm (so-called hot spots) in the local reaction system [99]. Practically, appropriately controlled ULS irradiation conditions play a vital role in the coordination reaction between the RE ions and ligand. Controlled acoustic cavitation was able to define the cycle (e.g., formation, growth, and collapse) of hot spot bubbles formed in the reaction solution [105,106]. The ULS waves can also play vital roles in the surface cleaning of RE-MOFs and activation of unsaturated metal active sites by rupturing their axial-coordinated bonds with solvent molecules [45]. In fact, the resonant cavitation bubbles in the ULS concept can also lead to the breakdown of particle aggregates during the synthesis of MOF crystals, helping to obtain them at nanoscale dimensions. For example, nano-sized [Tb(BTC) $(\mathrm{H}_{2}0)_{6}]_{\mathrm{n}}$ RE-MOF (yield of $0.6\mathrm{-}7.2\%$ with diameters of $50{-}100~\mathrm{nm}$ ) was synthesized by optimizing the ULS reaction time between 30 and $90~\mathrm{{min}}$ [105].
Electrochemical approach (Fig. 8) has been used as an emerging method for the growth of RE-MOF thin films. The properties of REMOF thin films (e.g., morphology, crystallinity, thickness, functionality, and surface roughness) can be modulated by controlling the electrosynthesis parameters (e.g., electrolyte constituents, current density, additive, and reaction time) [107]. Compared to the solvothermal synthetic methods, the electrochemical synthesis strategy for RE-MOF thin film offers various advantages (e.g., milder conditions, facile control on RE-MOF film characteristics, continuous reaction, and rapid processing (i.e., the possibility of scaling-up)) [108,109]. Electrochemical synthesis strategy can be categorized into three classes: electrophoretic deposition (EPD), anodic electrodeposition (AED), and cathodic electrodeposition (CED) [110,111]. In anodic electrodeposition (AED), the anode is often used as an in-situ source of RE $(\mathrm{Ln}^{3+})$ cations to grow REMOF crystals, either in a continuous flow cell or in batch mode. Accordingly, only RE-MOFs with the same anodic RE metal substrate can be synthesized [3]. Under an applied electrical potential, the dissolution of the anode (i.e., anodic oxidation) leads to the introduction of $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ hydroxide anions in an electrolyte solution containing organic ligand [112,113]. Hence, in the AED process, the coordination of organic ligands (in the electrolyte solution) with anodic oxidized RE ions allows the growth of RE-MOF thin film on a metal substrate placed near the electrode surface. In the CED approach, RE-metal ions are introduced in the electrolyte solution for the synthesis of RE-MOF thin films. Accordingly, the electrolyte anions (e.g., $\mathsf{N O}_{3}^{-}$ ) are reduced to generate hydroxide anions $(\mathrm{OH^{-}})$ which raise the pH value near the cathode surface. Based on this concept, the CED method relies on the deprotonation of carboxylate organic ligand through the accumulation of $\mathrm{\DeltaOH^{-}}$ ions via cathodic reduction of metal anions in the electrolyte solution [109]. Subsequently, the coordination of deprotonated organic ligands with RE metal ions in the electrolyte leads to the deposition of RE-MOF thin films on the cathode substrate [109,111]. Unlike AED or CED methods, the EPD is a two-stage electrochemical synthesis approach in which the charged RE-MOFs (pre-synthesized particles) in electrolyte suspension are deposited on the oppositely charged electrode under the influence of charge driven by an external electrical field [110]. Hence, the EPD of RE-MOF thin films is influenced by a number of variables like the zeta potential, conductivity, and particle size distribution of RE-MOFs in the electrolyte. An overview of these electrochemical synthesis methods, along with the corresponding parameters affecting the properties of MOF thin films, has been described previously [109,110].
The electrochemical synthesis method can initiate a continuous reaction without the need of anions in intermediate formation, which is beneficial for the production of the pure crystalline REMOF phase. However, at a high applied potential, the RE metal cation may get reduced and deposited on the cathode (i.e., metallic deposition as backside reaction). To avoid these limitations, alternative solvents (such as acrylates, acrylonitrile, and maleates) could be used as electrolyte solutions to prevent the reduction of metal cations [45]. Recently, a hybrid synthesis process has also been proposed to develop luminescent $\left[\mathrm{Ln}_{2}(\mathrm{TPO})_{2}(\mathrm{HCOO})\right](\mathrm{Me}_{2}-$ $\mathrm{NH_{2}})(\mathrm{DMF})_{4}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{6}$ MOF $\{{\mathrm{Ln}}={\mathrm{Tb}}\quad$ or Eu; $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{TPO}=$ tris-4-(carboxy phenyl) phosphine oxide} by combining electrochemical and microwave techniques, as shown in Fig. 8 [107]. In this protocol, lanthanide hydroxide $(\mathrm{Ln}(\mathrm{OH})_{3};$ ) layers were nucleated on the conducting glass electrode with fluorine-doped tin oxide (FTO). After the $\mathrm{Ln}(\mathrm{OH})_{3}$ layer formed on the FTO surface, the RE-MOFs film on the FTO substrate was synthesized under microwave irradiation $\cdot120\ s\mathrm{ec})$ using a $\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{T P O}$ ligand dissolved in a DMF solution. In this case, microwave radiation was applied to increase the seed number on the FTO support to avoid the back-side reduction of RE cations and the fast growth of RE-MOF crystals. Accordingly, a hybrid synthesis technique can be used as an attractive controlled synthesis procedure (instead of the traditional electrochemical approach) by controlling the thickness of the $\mathsf{L n}(\mathsf{O H})_{3}$ layer and microwave irradiation time.
A mechanochemical (i.e., solvent-free synthesis) strategy is another attractive eco-friendly method for the large-scale design of RE-MOF materials by employing large molecular weight organic linkers with poor solubility [15,102,112]. The use of this strategy was first reported for MOF engineering in 2006 by Pichon and co-workers [114]. Their method was based on the applied mechanical force/energy generated by fast grinding in a mechanical ball mill [115]. The high levels of applied mechanical energy can disrupt the intramolecular bonds in metal salts, followed by the reconstruction of new chemical bonds (metal complexes) between reactant molecules (metal cations and organic linkers) without any additive solvents. In some cases, a small amount of liquid can be added to accelerate mechanochemical reactions and produce polycrystalline RE-MOF products. For example, the solvent-free mechanochemical method has recently been utilized to synthesize various luminescent RE-MOFs by ball mill grinding at room temperature, such as $\begin{array}{r l}{{\mathrm{[Ln}}_{2}({\mathsf{B T C}})_{3}}&{{}}\end{array}$ ; $\mathrm{Ln}\ =\ G\mathrm{d}$ , Tb, and Dy] [15], $\mathtt{E u(L)_{n}}$ $\{\mathrm{L}\ =\ 4^{\prime}{,}4^{\prime\prime}$ -(1,4-phenylene)bis $(2,2^{\prime}{:}6^{\prime},2^{\prime\prime}$ -terpyridine)} [116], and $[\mathrm{Ln}_{2}(\mathsf{B D C})_{3}$ ; $\mathrm{Ln}=\mathsf{S m}$ , Gd, Eu, and Tb & BDC $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ benzene-1,4-dicar boxylic acid] [117]. To date, a general theory is not available to describe the physicochemical processes governing the mechanochemical synthesis. However, the most widely used models include the magma-plasma and hot-spot model [45]. The latter is based on the mechanochemical process based on the frictional contact between two solid surfaces under high temperatures $(>1,000^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ . In contrast, the former centers on the processes occurring at the contact point of colliding particles (temperature $>10^{4\circ}C$ at this spot) due to the quick formation of plasma (a large energy release) with the emission of free electrons. The main benefits of this synthesis approach include (1) high production yield of REMOF in a short reaction period, (2) no organic solvents are used, and (3) it is an economical procedure (energy-saving) for largescale production. However, it should be noted that some intermediate products may be formed during the mechanical reactions due to the existence of anionic salts in the reaction media. Such side reactions may exert a negative impact on the luminescent properties of RE-MOFs and related performances in medical applications.
The reverse microemulsion method has also been adopted as an effective and advanced route for the control synthesis of nanoscale RE-MOFs with desired properties (e.g., luminescence, shape, and morphology) for medical applications [76,118]. The microemulsion method (or surfactant-assisted solvothermal synthesis) is a controlled synthesis approach of nanoscale RE-MOFs in the emulsified liquid phase. The emulsified solution consists of organic solvent droplets in a water phase or water droplets in organic solvent commonly stabilized by surfactant molecules [21]. The surfactantstabilized microemulsion also provides thermodynamic means to increase the stability of nanoscale RE-MOF crystals in solution by avoiding agglomeration [20]. For example, a nano-scale MOF $\mathsf{\Pi}[\mathsf{E u}_{2}(-$ $\mathrm{Fum})_{2}(\mathrm{OX})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{4}$ : Fum $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ (E)-Butenedioic acid and $\mathrm{OX}=$ ethanedioic acid] was prepared by the water-in-oil microemulsion method assisted by cetyltrimethylammonium bromide (CTAB) surfactant at $150^{\circ}\mathsf C$ [119]. In this case, CTAB is used as a stabilizing and controlling agent to tune three-dimensional nanoscale RE-MOFs with different shapes based on the CTAB: water ratio. The shape of the RE-MOF $\mathrm{\Gamma[Eu_{2}(F M A)_{2}(O X)(H_{2}O)]}$ crystals changed from a rhombus-truncated bipyramidal morphology to elongated hexagonal nanoplates with an increase of the CTAB ratio from 15 to 20 wt $\%$ [119]. Using the same strategy, $\mathrm{Gd}(\mathrm{BDC})_{1.5}(\mathrm{H}_{2}0)_{2}$ nanorods were synthesized by dissolving $\mathsf{G d C l}_{3}$ and $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C}$ in a microemulsion system containing CTAB/isooctane/1-hexanol/water [120]. The size of the produced RE-MOF nanorods increased from the nanoscale $(\sim100-125\ \mathrm{nm}$ (length) and $40\ \mathrm{nm}$ (diameter)) to the microscale range $(\sim1-2~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ (length) and $100~\mathrm{{nm}}$ (diameter)) with increasing water ratio (from 5 to $10\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ . The observed alteration in REMOF particle size is likely due to the reduced number of nucleation sites (termed as nanoreactors: $500{-}1,000\ \mathrm{nm}$ in the microemulsion [73,120].
## 3.3. Controlled synthesis of nanoscale RE-MOFs
Numerous efforts have been put to control the synthesis of nanoscale luminescent RE-MOFs and their corresponding properties (e.g., morphology and surface functionality) to uplift their applicability in biomedical fields (e.g., sensing, drug delivery, and imaging). The preparation of nanoscale RE-MOFs is vital to enhance their penetrability into target cells for biomedical imaging and for improving the dispersion in different solvents [74]. In comparison to macroscale RE-MOFs, nanoscale RE-MOFs are also demonstrated with unique optical behaviors (e.g., high quantum yield and strong luminescent intensity with longer lifetime) [31]. Such uniqueness of nanoscale RE-MOFs is due to the quantum confinement effects (surface and interface interactions) induced by decreases in the size of the crystals relative to bulk RE-MOF [121]. These outstanding optical properties are important to improve the sensing abilities of RE-MOFs in living cells. In drug delivery, the high surface area of nanoscale RE-MOFs is also favorable to increase drug loading capacity [68].
In all synthesis strategies described above (sections 3.1 and 3.2), the size and shape of the RE-MOFs are controlled by the combination of multiple factors, including the nature of solvents, stoichiometry/concentration of the building block precursors, surfactant molar ratio, reaction temperature, solution pH, and reaction time. In addition, in the case of the electrochemical, microwave, and ultrasonic-assisted synthesis strategies, other physical conditions should also be considered for the construction of nanoscale RE-MOFs such as applied potential, electrode substrate, microwave energy, and ultrasonic power [122]. Amongst these parameters, the affinity between RE-metal ions and organic ligands is a critical parameter that controls the nucleation growth and morphology of nanoscale RE-MOFs [97]. A better solubility of reactants in the reaction solvent can also help increase the number of primitive nucleation sites, thereby allowing the uniform production of RE-MOF nanoparticles [31]. In addition, the concentration of emulsifier (surfactant) in the microemulsion synthesis method can act as a rate-limiting factor in the self-assembly of nanoscale REMOFs, as described in the previous section. In particular, the molar ratio between the dispersed phase (nanodroplet) and the surfactant (w) is vital to determine the basic properties (e.g., nucleation rate, growth kinetics, and morphology) of nanoscale RE-MOF crystals in the microemulsion method [123]. For instance, luminescent 3D Eu/Tb-MOFs nanoparticles $\mathrm{\mathop{Eu}}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{Tb}_{1-{\bf x}}$ –MOFs) were synthesized with an objective of developing pH sensor [124]. The reaction process (at $150^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $48\mathrm{{h}}$ ) proceeded in a solution mixture consists of CTAB: 1-hexanol: isooctane: water based on reveres (water-in-oil; W/O) microemulsion method [124]. The average dimensions of the prepared Eu/Tb-MOFs nanoplates were adjusted through the alteration of the W/O ratio. Specifically, the average dimensions (length/width $(\mathrm{in~nm})$ ) of Eu/TB-MOFs nanoplates decreased from 350/110 to 150/80 when the $\mathsf{W}/\mathsf{O}$ ratio was dropped from 20 to 10. Nonetheless, the pores of such nanoscale RE-MOFs are not completely free from the surfactant impurity, which is a main disadvantage of this method (Table 5).
Compared to the microemulsion method, the direct precipitation technique is a facile option to prepare nanoscale RE-MOFs under mild reaction conditions [121]. In this method, the building blocks (metal and ligand precursors) are first dissolved in an appropriate solvent. During the growth process, the precipitation of crystalline RE-MOF nanoparticles takes place in the original solvent as their crystal size exceeds a critical value [121]. If the formed RE-MOF crystals remain stable in the original solvents, a less solubilizing reagent or solvent may be added to accelerate the precipitation process. For example, a nanoscale RE-MOF $(\mathrm{Tb}_{2}\vert-$ $\mathrm{DSCP})_{3}(\mathrm{H}_{2}0)_{12}$ ; ${\mathrm{DSCP}}=$ disuccinatocisplatin) for anticancer drug delivery application was synthesized by a direct precipitation method [125]. The $\mathrm{Tb}_{2}(\mathrm{DSCP})_{3}(\mathrm{H}_{2}0)_{12}$ MOF nanoparticles (termed as NCP-1) were prepared by dissolving $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ ions and DSCP ligand in an aqueous solution (at $\mathsf{p H}5.5\mathsf{\cdot}$ ). The addition of methanol solvent to the above mixture allowed the precipitation of bluishwhite hue NCP-1 nanoparticles (size $58.3\pm11.3\mathrm{{\nm}}$ , yield $73\%$ at room temperature. Likewise, another nanocrystalline RE-MOF $\{\mathrm{La}(\mathrm{BTC})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{6}$ ; BTC $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ benzene-1,3,5-tricarboxylic acid} was prepared within $15~\mathrm{min}$ at room temperature via a simple precipitation method using water: ethanol solution mixture [126]. In this case, the micro/nanoscale size and morphological structures of La (BTC) $(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})_{6}$ MOF were proposed to be controlled by rational adjustments in the synthesis parameters (e.g., the molar ratio and concentration of metal ions and ligand, presence of additive (like PVP surfactant as a directing agent), and the ratio of the solvent mixture). Accordingly, the $\mathrm{La(BTC)(H}_{2}0)_{6}$ MOF nanoparticles with 3D flower-like morphology $(2-3~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ length) were obtained at lower reactant concentrations $0.05\mathrm{mmol}$ for BTC and ${\mathsf{L}}{\mathsf{a}}^{3+}$ ions) [126]. In contrast, a hierarchical straw-sheaflik structure with uniform nanorods $\mathrm{80}-120\ \mathrm{nm}$ width and $30{-}50~\mathrm{nm}$ thickness) was formed at elevated reactant concentrations (e.g., 1 mmol each) in the presence of PVP surfactant and keeping water: ethanol solution ratio of 3: 1 $(\boldsymbol{\mathsf{v}}/\boldsymbol{\mathsf{v}})$ . Further, as the molar ratio of BTC: ${\bf L}{\bf a}^{3+}$ ions decreased from 1: 1 to 1: 4 (e.g., through increasing ${\mathsf{L}}{\mathsf{a}}^{3+}$ ions to $4\mathrm{\mmol}\mathrm{\cdot}$ ), the morphology of the resulting La-MOF was changed from 3D-flower like architecture to spherical clusters $5~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ length) with smaller nanorods. These observations were accounted for by the ‘‘crystal splitting” mechanism due to the accelerated rates of crystal growth rates with increased reactant concentrations [126]. However, as the reactant concentrations exceeded a critical level, the crystal splitting mechanism was interrupted due to the accelerated growth rate of crystal seeds during the nucleation process. Such an accelerated kinetic growth rate can cause an increase in the aggregation of seeds, followed by their fusion to form larger macroscale particles. Similarly, the extent of the crystal splitting mechanism could also be inhibited in the presence of PVP surfactant, which affects the self-assembly mechanisms (i.e., nucleation and crystallization) of La-MOF. PVP surfactant was also found to play an important role as a stabilizing agent to prevent the aggregation of La-MOFs. Switching the ratio of water: ethanol solution mixture from 1: 1 to 3: 1 (v/v) also led to the alteration of La-MOF morphology from a 3D flower- to a taillike architecture [126]. This alteration may be explained by the effects of solution polarity or dipole–dipole interactions that can subsequently control the initial speciation of activator ions, kinetic growth of nanocrystals, and the progress of crystal splitting [122].
The use of capping agents with the ligands of similar chemical functionality has also been recommended to modulate the traditional solvothermal synthesis of nanoscale RE-MOFs. For example, a bimetallic RE-MOF thin film (e.g., $\mathsf{E u}_{1-\times}\mathrm{Tb}_{\mathrm{x}}$ -BTC MOF) was prepared by solvothermal treatment of building block precursors dissolved in water: DMF $(1\colon2{\mathrm{~}}\mathsf{v}/\mathsf{v})$ at $60~^{\circ}C$ for 12 to $^{72\mathrm{~h~}}$ in the presence of different sodium carboxylates as capping agents [127]. Based on the type of capping agents, the size and morphology of the prepared bimetallic RE-MOF crystals differed in terms of their ‘length $\times$ width’ characteristics as follows: (i) sodium formate $:125\pm25\mathrm{{nm}\times100\pm15\mathrm{{nm}}};$ , yielding bean-shaped nanocrystals, (ii) sodium acetate $(90\pm15\ \mathrm{nm}\times75\pm10\ \mathrm{nm})$ , yielding beanshaped nanocrystals, and (iii) sodium oxalate $(30-60~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ length), yielding needle-shaped micro-crystals. In this case, the capping agent regulates the RE-MOF crystal growth rate by modulating the coordinating interactions between the RE-metal ions and organic ligands. In the above study, it was also speculated that RE metal cations could coordinate with carboxylate moieties of both BTC ligand and carboxylic salt added during the early stage of crystal growth. Accordingly, the addition of an oxalate capping agent could interfere with the formation of pure $\mathsf{E u}_{1-\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{Tb}_{\mathrm{x}}$ -BTC MOF crystals due to the presence of dicarboxylate groups (as ligands) in sodium oxalate (relative to acetate and formate species).
Instead of carboxylate-based capping agents, amino acids can also be used as capping agents to prepare nanoscale RE-MOFs for molecular sensing applications. For instance, Tb-MOF-76 {Tb (BTC)(DMF) $)(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})\}$ was prepared by a microwave-assisted solvothermal approach (at $200~\mathsf{W}$ microwave power, $80~^{\circ}\mathsf{C},$ , and 30 min reaction) in the presence of proline and glycine amino acids as capping agents [128]. Using proline amino acid, the coordination sphere on $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ ions was modulated to reduce the dimensions (length) of pillar-like rods of Tb-MOF-76 crystals from $30\pm10~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ (in the absence of capping agent) to $100\pm25~\mathrm{{nm}}$ (at $1.0\ \mathrm{mmol}$ proline). Notably, the addition of $1.0\ \mathrm{mmol}$ proline also increased the solution pH from 3.98 to 5.58, demonstrating that both the capping agent and the acid-base environment can be used to interactively modulate the dimensions of RE-MOF crystals during the synthesis. Apart from the solvothermal approaches, a hybrid ultrasound-vapor phase diffusion method was applied for the fast-synthesis of $\left[\mathrm{Tb}(\mathsf{B T C})\right]_{\mathrm{n}}$ MOF nanocrystals under mild conditions within a short reaction period of ${<}140\ \mathrm{min}$ [106]. In this case, the nanostructured $\left[\mathrm{Tb}(\mathsf{B T C})\right]_{\mathrm{n}}$ ( $\mathsf{1}\mathrm{-}3\mathrm{~D~}$ structures with a yield up to $65.7\%$ was prepared using a vapor phase diffusion method in the presence of triethylamine (TEA) at $60~^{\circ}C$ within $2\mathrm{~-~}30\mathrm{~min}$ [106]. Under the ULS wave $(50~\mathrm{kHz})$ , the TEA vapor gradually diffused into the solution mixture (e.g., DMF, ethanol, and $\mathrm{H}_{2}{\boldsymbol{0}}^{\prime}$ to modulate the deprotonation of the $\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B T C}$ ligand. Thus, TEA played a crucial role in controlling the growth rate of $\left[\mathrm{Tb}(\mathsf{B T C})\right]_{\mathrm{n}}$ RE-MOF crystals and their coordination conditions.
From the above examples, it is thus concluded that the synthesis of nanostructured RE-MOFs for biomedical applications (e.g., with tunable sizes and well-defined morphologies) can be achieved through precise control on their nucleation and growth rates. In brief, the rational design of nanoscale RE-MOFs can be modulated by the following two main routes: (i) straightforward synthesis by careful selection of building blocks (metal ions and ligand precursors) and (ii) self-assembly synthesis be controlling operation parameters. Based on the synthesis methods, a slight change in the operating parameters can significantly alter both the dimensional and morphological properties of RE-MOFs. Such alteration may subsequently influence the performance of the obtained REMOFs toward sensing/biomedical applications. Accordingly, it is important to identify and optimize the key influence parameters that can effectively control the synthesis of nanoscale RE-MOFs [31,121,122,129].
## 3.4. Post-synthetic functionalization (PSF) methods
To date, enormous efforts have been made to control the design of RE-MOF hybrid materials for various sensing applications based on different PSF strategies (also termed as post-synthetic modification (PSM)) [94,95,130]. Based on the chemical modulation process, PSF strategies for the synthesis of luminescent RE-MOF hybrid materials are diverse enough to include: covalent bonding, dative (or coordinative) interactions, encapsulation (non-covalent) interactions, and tandem reactions. A detailed review has also been documented to describe the strategies for such PSF-based designs to construct diverse hybrid RE-MOFs [131].
In brief, dative PSF strategy is used widely to synthesize REcontaining hybrid MOF materials through the formation of a new coordination bond between RE-ions and the free functional groups in the ligand backbone of the MOF hosts. In this PSF strategy, RE ions (e.g., $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ and ${\mathsf{E u}}^{3+}$ ) are introduced as luminescent centers into porous MOFs to design new multifunctional RE-MOF hybrid materials with flexible structures. Notably, this strategy is also commonly used: (i) to overcome the incompatibility of some ligands to allow the synthesis of pure RE-MOFs and (ii) to avoid the possible occurrences of fluorescence quenching of RE-MOFs during pre-synthesis methods [131]. Accordingly, several multifunctional RE-MOF hybrid materials have been reported with unique properties of both pristine MOFs and the incorporated REions. Such modified RE-MOFs, with the aid of multiple luminescent RE-metal sites, exhibit an enhanced potential in the sensing of various analytes2018-2018. On the other hand, as a new modification strategy, a two-step tandem PSF was proposed for the controllable design of hybrid RE-MOFs with targeted functions that are difficult to be acquired via single-step dative PSF or pre-synthetic methods [132].
In comparison to the dative PSF strategy, only a few studies are available on the tandem PSF of MOFs to date. In tandem PSF, the first step is directed to introduce a new functionality (e.g., with uncoordinated active sites) to the ligand of the parent MOFs via covalent interaction under harsh conditions. In the second stage, RE-metal ions interact coordinatively with the new functionality so that the obtained functional RE-MOF hybrid materials exhibit high luminescence performance. Accordingly, the tandem interaction depends on the use of both covalent and coordinative PSF strategies. In fact, as a chemical modification method, covalent PSF is also used for enhanced sensitizing sensitivity of RE-MOFs frameworks through the covalent interaction of luminescent/fluorescent organic molecules with the functional ligand backbone [133,134]. As such, the tandem PSF strategy can be used to modify the structural properties of the parent MOFs, for example, in terms of functional, morphological (shape), textural (porosity and surface area), and mechanical (rigidity and flexibility) features of the framework. As an alternative technique to dative and tandem reactions, guest RE-metal cations or other luminescence molecules (like nanoparticles, quantum dots (QDs), or fluorescent dyes) can be incorporated within the channel structures (pores) of MOF hosts via encapsulation (or non-coordinative) PSF strategy. Such strategy is frequently employed in the absence of free available functional groups in the ligand backbone of parent MOFs for coordination with RE metal ions [135–137]. As such, for controllable design of hybrid RE-MOFs via PSF strategies, the selected MOF hosts should be characterized by (i) the availability of free available functionalities (e.g., COOH, $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ , or $S O_{3}\mathrm{H}$ ) to coordinate with RE ions, (ii) large and permanent porosity to allow successful entry of guest nanoparticles, and (iii) high chemical stability (robust framework) to prevent structural deformation of the framework during PSF process [95,138].
To address some examples of hybrid RE-MOFs built based on PSF strategies, a tandem PSF strategy was utilized to synthesize $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}@\mathrm{UiO-}66\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{-IM}$ for enhanced luminescent sensing of trace water and aniline molecules in the organic solvents (e.g., ethanol and DMF) [133]. In this work, imidazole-2-formaldehyde (IM) was covalently linked with the amine group of $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ through the Schiff-base reaction mechanism. Next, $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}@\mathrm{UiO}-66-$ $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -IM with a long fluorescence lifetime $1116.8\ \mathrm{ms})$ was produced through the coordinative reaction of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ metal cations with the open N sites of MI functionality. Likewise, luminescent MIL-125(Ti)- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -Eu hybrid material was prepared by covalent interaction of the amino groups in MIL-125(Ti)- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ with 2,3- pyridinedicarboxylic anhydride (with free N, O unsaturated sites). The above structure was subsequently coordinated with ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ metal cations [139]. The prepared MIL-125(Ti)- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ -Eu exhibited high fluorescent properties as a result of an effective charge transfer mechanism between the two luminescent centers in the modified frameworks (i.e., ${\sf E u}^{3+}$ sites and 2,3-pyridinedicarboxylic ligand).
Based on dative PSF, ${\sf E u}^{3+}$ metal sites were introduced into UiO66- $(\mathsf{C O O H})_{2}$ with the aim of developing $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}@\mathrm{UiO-}66\mathrm{-}(\mathrm{COOH})_{2}$ for fluorescent sensing of $\operatorname{Cd}(\operatorname{II})$ ions in water solution [140]. In this case, ${\mathsf{E u}}^{3+}$ ions were allowed to coordinatively interact with free available carboxyl moieties (unsaturated sites) in the ligand backbone of UiO-66- $(\mathsf{C O O H})_{2}$ via post-ligand coordination interaction mechanism. Likewise, dual-emitting $\mathsf{A g^{+}/E u^{3+}@U i O{-}66\mathrm{-}(C O O H)_{2}}$ composites were also prepared by coordinative interaction of carboxyl acid in UiO-66- $({\mathsf{C O O H}})_{2}$ framework with different contents of ${\sf E u}^{3+}$ and ${\sf A g}^{+}$ cations under hydrothermal conditions [141,142]. By tuning the molar ratios of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ and ${\sf A g}^{+}$ metal cations as luminescent centers, the obtained $\mathsf{A g^{+}/E u^{3+}@U i O{-}66\mathrm{-}(C O O H)_{2}}$ materials were utilized for fluorescent sensing of airborne formaldehyde [142] and hydrogen sulfide $(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{S})$ [141] molecules. Notably, the bipyridine group in UiO-67-bpydc (bpydc $=2{,}2{'}$ -bispyr idine- $^{.5,5^{\prime}}$ -dicarboxylic acid) has also been employed as a free Lewis basic site to coordinate with Ln metal (e.g., ${\sf E u}^{3+}$ ) sites via the dative PSF method [143,144]. The prepared $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}@\mathrm{Ui}0{-}67{-}$ bpydc materials were applied for the luminescent sensing of various volatile organic compounds [144]. Similarly, the synthesis of $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}/\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}67$ -bpydc was also carried out to realize the fluorescent detection of glutathione (GSH) [145]. Sulfoxide groups (e.g., $S0_{3}^{2-1}$ in the parent MOFs can also be utilized as active sites for developing functional RE-MOF hybrid materials based on the dative PSF method (or post-ligand coordination modification). For example, a series of $\mathrm{Ln}(\mathrm{III})@\mathrm{MOF}–\mathrm{SO}_{3}^{-}$ hybrid materials with high luminescent properties were prepared [95]. In this case, MOF$S0_{3}^{-}$ $\{[\mathsf{Z}\mathsf{n}_{3}(\mathsf{\textmu}_{3}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{O}\mathsf{H})_{3}(2\mathrm{-}\mathrm{stp})(\mathsf{b p y})1.5(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})](\mathrm{EtOH})\mathrm{-}(\mathsf{2}\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})\}_{\mathrm{n}}$ ; where $\mathrm{EtOH}=$ ethanol, $\mathsf{b p y}=2,2^{\prime}$ -bipyridne, and $2\cdot\mathsf{s t p}=2$ -sulfonyltereph thalate) was first prepared under solvothermal conditions in water/ethanol solution mixture at 80C for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ [95]. The asprepared MOF- $S0_{3}^{-}$ was then immersed in aqueous solutions containing Ln(III) ions $\mathrm{\DeltaLn}=\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ , $\mathsf{D y}^{3+}$ , and $\mathsf{S}\mathsf{m}^{3+}$ ) under stirring at room temperature for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ , followed by vacuum drying at 100C. Among the prepared hybrid materials, $\mathrm{Tb}(\mathrm{III})@\mathrm{MOF}{-}\mathsf{S O}_{3}^{-}\mathsf{w}$ as investigated as a fluorescence sensor for sensitive detection of trans, trans-muconic acid (tt-MA, as a urinary biomarker of benzene exposure) based on fluorescence quenching (low detection limit (LOD) of $0.1\ \mathrm{ppm};$ [95]. Likewise, a series of fluorescent $\mathrm{RE@La}-$ MOFs hybrid systems exhibited enhanced sensitivities toward acetaldehyde from various organic molecules (e.g., methanol, chloroform, formaldehyde, acetonitrile, DMF, ethanol, acetone, ethyl acetate, ether, and carbon tetrachloride) [94]. In this work, a pre-synthesized La-MOFs $(\{\mathrm{[La(hisp)(Hhisp)(H_{2}O)_{2}]{\cdot}2H_{2}O\}_{n}}$ ; $\mathrm{hisp}=5$ -hydroxyisophthalic acid) was immersed in aqueous solutions containing RE metal cations (e.g., ${\mathsf{L}}{\mathsf{a}}^{3+}$ , ${\sf E u}^{3+}$ , or $\mathrm{La}^{\bar{3}+}/\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}(1{:}1$ ratio)) for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ at room temperature. In terms of sensing response, Tb@La-MOF showcased the strongest luminescent emission (resulting from emission peak of ${}^{5}\mathrm{D}_{4}\ensuremath{\rightarrow}^{7}\mathrm{F}_{5}$ transition of $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ ions) with high sensitivity for acetaldehyde (LOD of $0.00058\%$ in water).
Other than dative and tandem PSF strategies, a simple encapsulation of $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ cations $(\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}=\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ , ${\sf E u}^{3+}$ , or $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}/\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ with different molar ratios) into the porous structures of the 2D Zn-MOF framework has been reported an alternative means to produce a new blue-emitting Zn-MOF hybrid materials [146]. In this case, the 2D $Z\mathrm{n}$ -MOF $\mathrm{'}[\mathrm{Zn}(\upmu\mathrm{-}\mathrm{L})(\upmu\mathrm{-}1,3\mathrm{-}\mathrm{dpp})]$ , where $\mathrm{\DeltaL}=4{,}4^{\prime}$ -oxy-bis(benzoic acid) and $1,3\mathrm{-dpp}\ =\ 1,3\mathrm{-}$ di(4-pyridyl)propane) was prepared by solvothermal method in DMF solvent at 140C for $^{72\mathrm{~h~}}$ . After that, $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ cations were encapsulated by soaking the obtained $Z\mathrm{n}$ -MOF (called mof-1) in aqueous $\mathrm{Ln}(\mathsf{N O})_{3}$ solutions $(\mathrm{Tb}^{3+},\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ , or $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}/$ ${\tt E u}^{3+})$ at room temperature for less than one hour, followed by washing and vacuum drying. The obtained $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}@\mathrm{Zn-MOF}$ hybrid materials exhibited multi-response light emissions (e.g., red, green, and nearly pure white light). In particular, by tuning the stoichiometry of $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ chromophores, the co-doped $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}/\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}@\mathrm{Zn}-$ MOFs showed unique thermal responsive luminescence (e.g., chromic transition from blue at liquid nitrogen temperature to white at room temperature). Accordingly, PSF strategies are expected to effectively incorporate RE metal sites into the parent MOFs with the objective to design multifunctional RE-MOFs hybrid materials with strong luminescence features for sensing applications.
## 4. Applications of RE-MOFs in biosensing and biomedical fields
RE-MOFs have been explored for a myriad of applications owing to their inherent fluorescent characteristics. The applications of REMOFs in other miscellaneous biomedical fields are summarized below.
## 4.1. Fluorescence and magnetic resonance imaging
Fluorescence imaging is an important branch of medical imaging technology due to its high sensitivity, time resolution, quick results, ease of use, and low cost. The unique luminescent properties of rare earth metal ions enable nano RE-MOFs to effectively detect biomolecules via fluorescence and MRI contrast imaging (Table 6). In one such report, near-infrared (NIR)–emitting $\Upsilon\mathsf{b}^{3+}$ - based nano-MOF (Yb-PVDC-3; PVDC $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ Poly(p-phenylene vinylene) dicarboxylate ligand)was introduced into living cells (HeLa and NIH 3 T3) as a reporter molecule for NIR imaging [89]. Yb-MOF showed high stability in biological media with no photobleaching and an $\mathsf{I C}_{50}$ of about $100~{\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}}$ , demonstrating its potential in live-cell imaging applications. Gd(III)-based MOFs (Gd-pDBI-1 and Gd-pDBI-2) using pDBI $\mathrm{{'}p D B I}=1{,}4$ -bis(5-carboxy-1H-benzimi dazole-2yl)benzene) have also been reported for fluorescence and MR imaging [78].
Likewise, a series of RE-MOF crystals with $\mathrm{Ln}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ $\mathrm{(Ln=La^{3+}}$ , ${\mathsf{N}}{\mathsf{d}}^{3+}$ , ${\sf E u}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ , and $\mathrm{Er}^{3+}$ ) and $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -thiodiacetic acid (TDA) were synthesized based on a lithium intercalation method for imaging applications (Fig. 9) [147]. Note that these RE-MOFs had different crystal structures with respect to the corresponding $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ ions (Fig. 9). However, all of their unit cell structures were mainly coordinated by one sulfur atom and nine oxygen atoms. The prepared crystals were stripped into ultra-thin nanosheets over a prolonged reaction time (up to $20~\mathrm{{~h~}},$ and their fluorescence-quenching properties were evaluated using fluorescent dye-labeled single-stranded DNA (ssDNA) aptamer molecules.
As the fluorophore-labeled ssDNA was adsorbed on the Ln-MOF surfaces, their fluorescence was partially quenched due to charge transfer (e.g., from the fluorescent molecules to the lanthanum ions). Also, the quenching efficiency of dye molecules on Ln-MOF nanosheets decreased from ${\mathsf{L}}{\mathsf{a}}^{3+}$ to $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ due to the smaller radius of $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ ions. La-MOF showed the highest quenching efficiency compared to smaller ions $({\mathsf{N d}}^{3+}$ , ${\sf E u}^{3+}$ , and $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}.$ ), as confirmed by X-ray photoelectron and UV–visible spectroscopy. This is likely to be accounted for by the large size of ${\mathsf{L}}{\mathsf{a}}^{3+}$ ions, which should have facilitated energy transfer between the RE metal sites and fluorescent dye molecules that are in close vicinity. Fluorescence quenching was hence favorably induced by the shorter distance in energy transfer (Wang et al. 2017a).
A dye-labelled P2 aptamer/RE-MOF nanosheet complex was designed for the intracellular two-color fluorescence imaging of ATP activity in living cells [147]. Firstly, the La-MOF nanosheets were incubated together with equimolar concentrations of negatively charged TAMRA-labeled aptamer (TAMRA-P2) and positively charged FAM-labeled aptamer (FAM-P2) molecules to yield a dye${\mathsf{P}}2+$ MOF-La complex. The dye-labelled P2 aptamers bind with ATP to form a duplex configuration. The duplex formation leads to conformational changes in the aptamer structure, resulting in their detachment from the surface of the La-MOF nanosheets. However, the ATP-bound FAM-P2 molecules were observed to reattach to the edges of the MOF (e.g., through electrostatic interaction between $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ and FAM). Thus, the fluorescence of the FAM-P2/MOF complex was quenched while the fluorescence of TAMRA-P2 was recovered. The developed dye-P2 $^+$ MOF-La biosensor was also employed for sensing adenosine activity in living cells using confocal microscopy. After the interaction with intracellular ATP, the fluorescence color was altered to reddish-orange from kelly green. The biosensor showed a low detection limit of $500~\mathsf{p M}$ along with a linear detection range of 0.5 to $15~\mathrm{{nM}}$ Thus, it was suggested that LnMOF (or RE-MOFs) nanosheets could be used for the two-color detection of intracellular DNA and bioimaging in living cells [147].
Fluorescence bioimaging is more preferably employed in an in vitro than in vivo imaging due to the lack of detailed information about anatomy and physiology in vivo. In addition, in vivo applications in biological fluids become even more challenging as most of the contents therein (e.g., protein, enzyme, and DNA) are electronrich molecules [148]. These biomolecules can absorb the externally provided excitation energy (e.g., the wavelength region of 200– $380~\mathrm{{\nm}}.$ , followed by photoinduced electron transfer (PET)- governed emission at longer wavelengths. This light absorptionemitting phenomenon is called photoluminescence. Accordingly, the photoluminescence characteristics of RE-MOFs induced by UV-light can be subject to the possible interfering effects of the co-existing biomolecules. For instance, it can be assumed that the absorption of photon energy takes place simultaneously by the co-existing biological species upon photo-excitation (under UV light). These biomolecules are then expected to lower the sensitivity of the fluorescence imaging system (e.g., false-negative response), as there is a reduction in the net energy available for the photoexcitation of RE-MOF-based sensors. In other words, the photophysical characteristics of RE-MOFs (in terms of quantum yield, Stokes shift, and molar extinction coefficient) in the biological fluid media can diminish significantly in in-vivo imaging due to the co-existing (interfering) biomolecules. Besides, RE-MOFs may reabsorb the emitted light from biomolecules upon excitation under UV-light. Such interfering effects may also limit the sensitivity and selectivity of the fluorescent probes (e.g., false-positive colored imaging) in real bioimaging fields to a large extent.
As the luminescent features of RE-MOFs are the key criterion for fluorescent probes, it is thus crucial to address the role of the photoluminescence interference from background biological species during in vivo imaging. Hence, to overcome these drawbacks, it is important to develop photoluminescent RE-MOFs with improved and tunable upconversion emission under visible or near-infrared light excitation to avoid false-negative/positive responses [41,149]. However, in some cases, the nonspecific interactions between the RE-MOFs and the surrounding biomolecules may also exert a negative impact on photostability (or increased photobleaching), thereby reducing the fluorescence lifetime (e.g., with a false-negative detection) [150,151]. Therefore, the fluorescence sensing applications of RE-MOFs based on PET mechanism have not been successful in real bioimaging fields. More importantly, it should be noted that fluorescent imaging suffers from the limited depth of penetration in the three spatial dimensions (3D) because there is no specific contrast for each internal organ in this imaging modality [152,153]. Accordingly, fluorescence bioimaging is not able to detect small tumor cells accurately in internal organs at the early stages of carcinogenic diseases.
The above limitation was overcome by the development of MRI as it can provide extensive information with respect to the anatomical and/or physiological status (e.g., good soft tissue contrast) [153]. MRI has received extensive attention due to its noninvasive properties, sub-millimeter spatial resolution, high anatomical contrast, and excellent soft-tissue differentiation ability [154]. However, the images obtained by MRI are sometimes insufficient to distinguish between normal and damaged tissues as the contrast agents can have therapeutic effects on the defect. To date, the combination of MRI and fluorescence imaging has been proposed as a useful tool for bio-imaging. The combination of fluorescence imaging and MRI technologies has demonstrated many advantageous properties such as discernible contrast (between damaged and normal tissue), high depth of tissue penetration, and high sensitivity [155,156]. Therefore, it is very important to develop imaging agents with dual-mode $\mathrm{T}_{1}\mathrm{-}\big/\mathrm{T}_{2^{-}}$ weighted MRI imaging capabilities, which is useful to improve image saliency and diagnostic sensitivity while minimizing the risk of blurring [157–159].
For the development of dual-mode imaging, nanoscale RE-MOFs consisting of a fluorescent organic linker and paramagnetic metal ions (e.g., ${\mathsf{G}}{\mathsf{d}}^{3+}.$ ) have been proposed as a potential solution for advanced image contrast to increase water proton relaxation rates [120]. For instance, the synthesis of luminescent $\mathrm{Gd}(\mathrm{BDC})_{1.5}(\mathrm{H}_{2}0)_{2}$ nanorods was carried out via the inverse microemulsion method using ${\mathsf{E u}}^{3+}$ or $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ dopants (Fig. 10) [120]. Relaxivity studies of $\mathrm{Gd}(\mathrm{BDC})_{1.5}(\mathrm{H}_{2}0)_{2}$ nanorods exhibited a longitudinal relaxation degree $\left(r_{1}\right)$ of ${\sim}1.6\times10^{7}\ s^{\mathrm{~-~}1}$ and a transverse relaxation degree $(r_{2})$ of ${\sim}2.5\times10^{7}\ s^{-1}$ per mmol of RE-MOF nanorods. The level of relaxivity by $\mathrm{Gd}(\mathrm{BDC})_{1.5}(\mathrm{H}_{2}0)_{2}$ was an order of magnitude larger than that of liposomes containing $\mathsf{G d}^{3+}$ . Thus, $\mathrm{Gd}(\mathrm{BDC})_{1.5}(\mathrm{H}_{2}0)_{2}$ can be employed as an efficient target-specific MR contrast agent for cardiovascular and cancer diseases. Also, RE-MOF nanorods doped with ${\sf E u}^{3+}$ or $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ ([Gd0.95 Eu0.05 $\mathrm{(BDC)}_{1.5}(\mathrm{H}_{2}0)_{2}]$ and $[\mathrm{Gd}_{0.95}\mathrm{Tb}_{0.05}(\mathrm{BDC})_{1.5}(\mathrm{H}_{2}0)_{2}])$ display red and green luminescence, respectively, upon UV excitation. As such, these observations seem to support the feasibility of utilizing RE-MOFs for multimodal imaging. Further, a nanoscale $\mathrm{Gd}(1,2,4\mathrm{-BTC})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{3}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ MOF was synthesized using a reverse microemulsion process by incorporating hydrotropes such as sodium salicyclate, salicyclic acid, and 5-methyl salicyclic acid [79]. The synthesized nano-MOF was used as an effective alternative to positive contrast agents for MRI in an in vitro MRI scanner. The effects of different parameters such as surface area, shape, size, and length distribution on the relaxivity potential of the MOF were evaluated in their work. A strong correlation between the longitudinal relaxivity values and the Gd-MOF surface area was observed. There was an increase of the $r_{1}$ value (e.g., from 17.8 to $70.9\ \mathrm{mM}^{-1}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1};$ in comparison to clinically used Magnevist $(4.4\ \mathrm{mM}^{-1}\ s^{-1}).$ . The maximum obtained $r_{1}$ value $\mathrm{\langle83.9\mM^{-1}\ s^{-1}},$ was about a factor of 20 larger than that of Magnevist. It was inferred that Gd-MOFs with a higher surface area should provide more $\mathsf{G d}^{3+}$ ions to coordinate with water molecules, thereby increasing the relaxivity [79].
Similarly, a 3-D microporous RE-MOF, $\mathsf{I G d C u(D O T P)C l l}].$ $.5\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ (also called CAMOF-1), was constructed by binding $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}/\mathsf{G d}^{3+}$ to a macrocyclic chelating ligand, DOTP (1,4,7,10-tetraazacyclodode cane-1,7,10-tetramethylenephosphonic acid), which possessed enhanced stability in a physiological saline solution [160]. The 3- D structure of the RE-MOF was attributed to the chelation of ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ ions (inside the MOF cage) to the DOTP ligand. Also, the $\mathsf{G d}^{3+}$ ions formed coordinate bonds with the phosphonate groups of DOTP to maintain the structural integrity of RE-MOF. The potential of CAMOF-1 as a contrast agent was studied using $^1\mathrm{H\mathrm{\cdot}}$ -NMRD under various magnetic field and temperature conditions. The longitudinal relaxivity $(r_{1})$ reached a maximum value of $15\mathrm{\mM}^{-1}\mathrm{\s}^{-1}$ (at ${400}\mathrm{MHz}\mathrm{,}$ at a temperature of $25^{\circ}\mathsf C$ . The $r_{1}$ value was $6\mathrm{mM}^{-1}$ $s^{-1}$ at $37^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ , even at a high magnetic field of ${500}\mathrm{MHz}$ . A proportional increase of the relaxivity values was observed with increasing temperature due to the possibility of proton exchange between the nano-wired RE-MOF and bulk water molecules [160]. The utility of another microporous nanorod-structured MOF was proposed in bimodal imaging for two mammalian cell lines, HEK293T and H1299 [161]. A 3-D nano-RE-MOF (NCP-1) $\{[\mathrm{Gd}(\mathrm{OPE})(\mathrm{NO}_{3})$ $(\mathrm{H}_{2}0)_{2}]{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}0\}$ was synthesized using a self-assembled coordination network of paramagnetic $\mathsf{G d}^{3+}$ ions and organic ligand OPE [oligo( $\dot{p}$ -phenyleneethynylene)dicarboxylate] in a polar solution. The nanometric size and strong luminescent properties (blue and cyan emission) of the NCP-1 were examined for its internalization into the cell lines for imaging applications using immunofluorescence. Moreover, the MOF characteristics, such as the presence of MRI active $\mathsf{G d}^{3+}$ ions, reduced cytotoxicity, and high stability, have contributed to its use as a negative contrast agent $\left(\mathsf{T}_{2}\right)$ in MRI. The values of $r_{1}$ $(0.3\mathrm{\mM}^{-1}\mathrm{\s}^{-1})$ and $r_{2}$ $\mathsf{\Omega}^{27.9\mathrm{\mM}^{-1}\mathrm{\Omega}s^{-1})}$ indicate high transverse relaxivity due to the coordination interactions between $\mathsf{G d}^{3+}$ and water [161].
Recently, nano RE-MOFs of Eu and Gd-MOFs were proposed for application as MRI agents (Fig. 11). This RE-MOF was prepared in large quantities by dissolving Res (e.g., ${\sf G d}({\sf N O}_{3})_{3}.6{\sf H}_{2}0$ and Eu $(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}0)$ as metallic nodes and benzene-1,3-dicarboxylic acid (or isophthalic acid: $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{IPA}^{\cdot}$ ) as an organic linker in the presence of PVP and HMTA as regulating agents [71]. The obtained fluorescent nano-Eu, Gd-MOFs were further modified by a silica coating (Eu, Gd-NMOFs $\varpi\mathsf{S i O}_{2}$ ) to enhance the relaxation degrees $\cdot\boldsymbol{r}_{1}$ and $r_{2}$ values of 38 and $222\ \mathrm{mM}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ , respectively). As such, the results indicate the strong potential of a dual-modal $\mathrm{T}_{1}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{T}_{2}$ contrast agent. The reason behind the increased $r_{1}$ and $r_{2}$ values can be accounted for by the rigid confinement of $\mathsf{G d}^{3+}$ ions in the nano-MOF structure, which led to slow interexchange of $\mathsf{G d}^{3+}$ with water molecules [162]. Cytotoxicity studies of Eu, Gd${\mathsf{N M O F@S i O_{2}}}$ nanoparticles $(20~\upmu\mathrm{M}~\mathsf{G}\mathsf{d}^{3+}.$ on U87MG cells showed no detectable cytotoxicity, demonstrating good biocompatibility. Also, cell uptake studies were conducted by conjugating the Eu, ${\mathsf{G d}}{\mathsf{-N M O F@S i O_{2}}}$ nanoparticles with cRGDyK (a cyclic peptide exhibiting a high binding affinity against integrin $\mathsf{\alpha}\mathsf{\alpha}\mathsf{\beta}_{\mathsf{3}}$ ) [163]. The results of in vitro studies indicated the efficient internalization of RGD-Eu, Gd-NMOF $\varpi\mathsf{S i O}_{2}$ nanoparticles, as visualized by MRI and fluorescence microscopy. Similar to the in vitro studies, in vivo injection of RGD-Eu, $\mathbf{Gd{-}N M O F@{S i}O_{2}}$ into $\mathsf{B A L B}/\mathrm{c}$ mice confirmed the potential of nano RE-MOFs as a dual-mode contrast agent (Fig. 11) [71]. In another report, a nanocomposite of GdMOF/gold nanoparticles (AuNPs) was prepared using poly (acrylic acid) as bridge molecules for MRI/CT bimodal imaging applications [164]. The study demonstrated that the presence of unpaired electrons and the large magnetic moment of gadolinium $(\mathsf{G}\mathsf{d}^{3+})$ in the RE-MOF nanocomposite affected its water relaxation properties responsible for contrasting effects in MRI. Also, the high X-ray attenuation coefficient and the atomic number of gold in a composite make it an excellent contrast agent for X-ray-based CT imaging applications. The developed hybrid nanocomposite demonstrated high performance (e.g., in terms of longitudinal relaxivity in MRI and excellent CT imaging).
## 4.2. Drug delivery and cancer therapy
The encapsulation of drug molecules into RE-MOF-based nanocarriers has been successfully employed to increase the permeability of biofilms, change the distribution in vivo, regulate the rate of drug release, and improve stability/bioavailability. Compared to many other carriers (e.g., liposomes, dendrimers, micelles, mesoporous silicon, and inorganic nanoparticles), RE-MOFs have some of the following unique advantages as pharmaceutical carriers [38,165].
(1) They can be simulated and designed according to the drug characteristics. Their compositions and architecture geometries can be varied.   
(2) RE-MOFs have an affinity to effectively capture drug molecules with high loading doses due to their large pore volume-to-surface area ratios.   
(3) Imaging-mediated drug quantification, targeted cell delivery, and cancer treatments can be achieved by exploiting the unique optical and magnetic features.
(4) RE-MOFs can be rationally functionalized with upconversion luminescence (UCL) to serve as dual-mode drug carriers and contrast imaging agents with improved accuracy for disease diagnosis and treatment.
For instance, heme protein cytochrome $c$ (Cyt c) molecules were successfully immobilized into a Tb-based MOF consisting of meso- $\cdot/$ micro-porous cages and windows (Tb-mesoMOF) (Fig. 12) [166]. During the encapsulation process, the conformation of the Cyt c protein was altered from its native form accompanied with a subsequent decrease of the surface area from $1,935\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~g~}^{-1}$ to $348~\mathrm{m}^{2}$ $\mathrm{~g~}^{-1}$ . This reduction was ascribable to the larger dimensions of Cyt c than the Tb-MOF porous sizes. The fluorescence of Tb-meso MOFs was suggested to be used as a new delivery vehicle for biological macromolecules or medical cargo (e.g., therapeutic and imaging agents). For example, silica shell-coated $\mathrm{\sfPt}$ -containing nano REMOFs $\mathrm{[Tb_{2}(D S C P)_{3}(H_{2}O)_{12}]}$ $\{\mathrm{DSCP}=\mathrm{c,c,t}\cdot$ -(diamminedichlo-rodisuc cinato) $\mathsf{P t}(\mathsf{I V})$ or disuccinatocisplatin} have been formulated (Fig. 13) to control the rapid dissolution and release of a Pt drug to cancer cells [167]. The anticancer performance of Pt-based TbMOFs (NCPs: nanoscale coordination polymers) under in vitro conditions was also evaluated using multiple cell lines (HT-29 and MCF-7). The treatment of HT-29 with DCSP or NCPs did not produce any toxic effects on cancer cells due to a lack of a pathway for entry inside cells under in vitro conditions. Thus, the NCPs were conjugated with silyl-derived cRGDfK (a cyclic peptide sequence with a high affinity for the $\mathsf{\alpha}\mathsf{\alpha}\mathsf{\beta}_{\mathsf{3}}$ integrin that is upregulated in angiogenic tumor cells, HT-29) for efficient cell uptake. The $\mathrm{IC}_{50}$ values of the RGD-NCPs were found to be lower $(9.7\mathrm{~-~}11.9\mathrm{~\textmuM})$ 1 compared to the standard cisplatin drug $\upmu\mathrm{M})$ , suggesting sufficient internalization of targeted-NCPs (Fig. 8). Also, in vitro cell cytotoxicity assays were conducted on human breast carcinoma cell lines (MCF-7) using Pt-NCPs and a standard cisplatin drug. In contrast to HT-29, the introduction of Pt (IV) into NCPs was found to be effective against MCF-7 due to the absence of overexpressed $\upalpha_{\mathrm{v}}\upbeta_{3}$ integrin in MCF-7 cell lines. The $\mathrm{IC}_{50}$ values of both NCPs and cisplatin were equal in this case. This work demonstrated that NCPs can be employed as potential drug delivery vehicles for cancer treatment.
The unique properties of nano RE-MOFs to allow the integration of diagnostic/therapeutic reagents has received extensive attention from researchers. For instance, nanoscaled theragnostic devices were successfully constructed by the surface modification of an MRI contrast agent (Gd-MOF) with co-polymers of poly(N-isopro pylacrylamide)-co-poly(N-acryloxysuccinimide)-co-poly(fluores cein O-methacrylate) for multifunctional drug delivery and tumor therapy (Fig. 14) [90]. The polymer-modified Gd-MOF was employed as a scaffold for the thiolate-assisted attachment of antitumor drug molecules (methotrexate; MTX) and the targeting ligand (H-glycine-arginine-glycine-aspartate-serine- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ peptide; ${\mathrm{GRGDS-NH}}_{2}$ ). The vacant orbitals of the $\mathsf{G d}^{3+}$ ions were helpful in the conjugation process of drug/ligand to the Gd-MOF nanoparticles (Fig. 15) [168,169]. The relaxivity values of the Gd-MOF modified with polymers increased considerably $(r_{1}=33.4~\mathrm{mM}^{-1}s^{-1})$ 1 relative to its unmodified form $(r_{1}=9.86~\mathrm{mM}^{-1}s^{-1}.$ ). The modified Gd-MOF exhibited a three-fold enhancement due to increased water retention using the hydrophilic copolymer matrix attached to the surface of the Gd-MOF. Also, the $\mathsf{G R G D S–N H}_{2}$ polymermodified Gd-MOF nanoparticles were found to be an active targeting platform for cancer cells (such as FITZ-HAS sarcoma cell line expressing $\upalpha_{\mathrm{v}}\upbeta_{3.}$ .-integrins), as demonstrated by fluorescence microscopy. In addition, the polymer-modified Gd-MOF conjugated with $\mathsf{M T X/G R G D S–N H_{2}}$ demonstrated growth inhibition of FITZ-HSA tumor cells, as confirmed by cell viability measurements. These results support the feasibility of using nano rare earth MOFs to integrate imaging, targeting, and therapeutic capabilities into a single nanoscale diagnostic platform. Thus, a nanoscale theragnostic device was developed with dual-targeted contrast imaging and therapeutic properties (Fig. 15) [90].Fig. 16.
The use of RE-MOFs in photodynamic therapy (PDT) has been proposed for effective anticancer treatments based on the generation of cytotoxic reactive oxygen species (ROS). One such report demonstrated the application of a Hf–porphyrin nanoscale REMOF (DBP–UiO) for tumor eradication in the head and neck using PDT [170]. The advantages of using UiO-NMOF in PDT included efficient production of singlet oxygen species $({}^{1}0_{2})$ because of intersystem crossing by heavy Hf centers, site isolation of porphyrin ligands in the RE-MOF structure, and facile diffusion of $^10_{2}$ through the porous structure of DBP–UiO nanoplates. In another report, a radioactive UiO-66 nMOF $^{89}\mathrm{Zr}$ -UiO-66) was functionalized with pyrene-derived polyethylene glycol (Py–PGAPEG) and conjugated with a peptide ligand (F3) for the imaging of triple-negative breast tumors [171]. Also, doxorubicin (DOX) was embedded inside the porous structure of UiO-66 with a loading capacity of $1\ \mathrm{mg\D0X/mg}$ UiO-66. Accordingly, $^{89}\mathrm{Zr}$ -UiO-66/
Py–PGA-PEG-F3 can be used as an effective nanoplatform for image-guidable, tumor-selective cargo delivery.
A $\mathsf{G d}^{3+}$ co-doped $\mathrm{Tm}^{3+}$ MOF $\mathbf{\langleGd/Tm-MOF\rangle}$ was also reported for concurrent applications toward dual-imaging and drug delivery [172]. The porous $\operatorname{Gd}/\operatorname{Tm}$ -MOFs modified with folic acid (FA) and uniform mesoporous silica $\mathrm{(mSiO_{2})}$ shells exhibited a noticeable enhancement of the drug (e.g., DOX) loading capacity from 10.6 to $41.5~\mathrm{mg/g}$ . The DOX release was around $30\%$ at $\mathsf{p H}5.8$ for the first ten hours. The DOX release reached a maximum of $64\%$ at $\mathsf{p H}~5.8$ , while it was only $12\%$ at $\mathsf{p H}~7.4$ , demonstrating a pHresponsive drug release property. The presence of paramagnetic Gd with Tm in the MOF structure enabled its use for UCL and MRI dual-mode imaging with a longitudinal relaxivity $\left(\mathbf{r}_{1}\right)$ of $225.86\ \mathrm{\mM}^{-1}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ . Nanocomposites of MOF with NPs have also been reported recently for diverse biomedical applications (e.g., cancer cell imaging and treatment as well as drug delivery) [41]. A multifunctional, dual-color emitting, and water-soluble nanocomposite of NaLnF $_4\textcircled{\theta}$ MOF-Ln was prepared by hybridization of a mesoporous RE-MOF ${\mathrm{~:}}{\mathrm{Y}}^{3+}$ : ${\sf E u}^{3+}$ -MOF) with luminescent ${\sf N a Y F}_{4}$ : $\mathrm{Tm}^{3+}/\mathrm{Yb}^{3+}$ nanoparticles. The material was demonstrated as a carrier for the delivery of an anticancer drug (e.g., DOX) in simulated cancer cell environments. The hybrid nanocomposite showed good biocompatibility and dual-color fluorescence for efficient diagnosis and treatment of tumors. In another work, mixed NCPs consisting of $\mathsf{G d}^{3+}$ and $\Upsilon\mathsf{b}^{3+}$ with a $\mathrm{Ru}[4,4^{\prime}{-}(\mathrm{COOH})_{2}\mathrm{bpy}]$ $\mathbf{\tau}_{3}{\cdot}{\mathbf{Cl}}_{2}$ ligand were employed for the tri-modal imaging-guided PDT [173]. $\mathsf{G d}^{3+}$ provided MRI characteristics while $\Upsilon\mathsf{b}^{3+}$ and Ru produced $\mathsf{X}$ -ray attenuation and red fluorescence, respectively. Triple modality imaging, X-ray computed tomography (CT), and PDT were efficiently realized by using NCPs as the probe. Likewise, the potential of a $\mathsf{G d}^{3+}$ -based nanoscale MOF synthesized using an organic ligand, BBDC (5-boronobenzene-1,3 dicarboxylic acid), was explored as an MRI guided drug delivery and chemotherapy agent. The biocompatibility of this MOF was enhanced by coating it with a layer of glucose. This nano-MOF was loaded with DOX anticancer drug using diol-borate condensation and then employed for the target recognition of glucose transporter proteins (GLUT1) overexpressed in cancer cells. The tumor targeting ability of the MOF was improved through an EPR (enhanced permeability and retention) effect. The proposed method was employed to develop a smart nanotherapeutic method for the leakage-free delivery of DOX [174]. The reports of RE-MOFs for drug delivery and cancer treatment applications are summarized in Table 7.
## 4.3. Sensing and other medical applications
## 4.3.1. Biosensing
Photoluminescent RE-MOFs have been frequently utilized for sensing various targets (e.g., cations/anions and small biomarkers) in biological fluids. Table 8 summarizes the performance of some RE-MOFs as fluorescent/luminescent probes for biosensing applications.
Luminescent sensing functions on the basis of changes in the photophysical characteristics of the RE-MOFs after their interactions with the target analyte or after alteration in the surrounding environmental parameters (e.g., temperature, pH, and pressure) [148]. Based on the electronic/ionic nature of the analyte being detected, the luminescent sensing can proceed by either quenching (turn-off) or boost (turn-on) in the emission intensity of RE-MOFs [175]. For both these types of sensors, the selected RE-MOFs should preferably exhibit the characteristic of high photostability, selectivity, and sensitivity.
For biosensing applications, the development of luminescence RE-MOF-based fluorescent/luminescent probes is often challenging due to the complex nature of biological fluids. As already mentioned in this review, the electron-rich nature biomolecule analytes (such as DNA, proteins, and enzymes) can undesirably contribute toward autofluorescence as they tend to absorb/emit light via the PET mechanism (e.g., donating electrons to the excited states upon excitation with UV light) [148]. Accordingly, such background signals can interfere with the photoluminescent features of RE-MOFs, particularly during the development of ‘‘turn-on” sensors. Because of this limitation, the researchers have preferred the biosensing applications of RE-MOFs in the ‘‘turn-off” mode type of signaling. However, in these sensors also, the use of UV light as excitation energy can lead to its simultaneous absorption by the biomolecules [176]. Consequently, it might lower the photoluminescence response and sensitivity of RE-MOFs sensors as there is less total energy available to allow optimal charge transfer (e.g., the ligand to metal, or antenna effect). Besides, in certain cases, the use of ‘‘turn-off” RE-MOF biosensors for the analysis of real biological fluids is likely to encounter the interference (lower emission intensity) from paramagnetic cations having d-d absorption bands (such as ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ , $\mathsf{M n}^{2+}$ , and $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ ), which are well-known as luminescence quenching ions [177,178]. Therefore, it is somehow challenging to develop luminescent RE-MOF biosensors with the desired sensitivity and selectivity as the real biological media would contain many interfering components. The researchers have proposed the use of RE-MOFs for the selective recognition of analyte in biological media based on different modes of luminescence quenching phenomena (e.g., fluorescence resonance energy transfer (FRET), inner filter effect (IFE), and dynamic quenching effect (DQE)) [148,176]. The detailed information on these mechanisms has been discussed in some of the previously published reviews [148,179–181].
As a biological sensing application example, a microporous EuMOF, $[\mathrm{Eu}_{2}(\mathrm{Fum})_{2}(\mathrm{ox})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{4}]{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , was adopted for the luminescent detection of $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}(10^{-2}\mathsf{M})$ in aqueous solutions [182]. Its high selectivity was attributed to the presence of two organic linkers ((E)-Butenedioic acid and ethanedioic acid) with good selectivity/ sensitivity for coordination with ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ ions. During the analysis of ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ , its incorporation in Eu-MOF caused significant quenching of the luminescence $\mathrm{(Ksv=528.7\M^{-1}}.$ ) with a visible change of the red color intensity (in UV light). Accordingly, for the first time, these results indicate that the application of such Eu-MOF-based sensors for the diagnosis or treatment of Wilson’s and Alzheimer’s disease is feasible through facile and real-time trace-level detection of ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ in the brain. Likewise, a fluorescent Eu-MOF containing IPA linker (Eu-IPA MOF) was explored for the development of a turn-on sensor for ${\mathrm{Hg}}^{2+}$ in biological fluid samples [183]. In this case, imidazole (Imd) was used as the fluorescent absorber in the sensor to filter out the excitation energy of Eu-MOFs based on the inner filter effect. The fluorescence of the Eu-IPA MOF was quenched in the presence of Imd due to the overlap between the excitation spectrum (Eu-MOFs) and the absorption spectrum (Imd absorber). However, the fluorescence was restored when $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ was poured into the solution because the formation of a stable $\mathrm{\sfHg/Imd}$ complex suppressed the inner filter effect of Imd. The developed sensor demonstrated high selectivity and sensitivity (e.g., LOD of $2{\mathrm{~nM}}$ for the fast detection of $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ in biological fluids such as urine samples.
It is known that the presence of dipicolinic acid (DPA) in the spores of certain bacteria is considered as a unique biomarker for their detection. On this basis, since RE ion- DPA complexes can generate optical signals to facilitate DPA detection, many reports were published for the selective detection of DPA using $\mathrm{Eu/Tb\mathrm{-}}$ based MOFs [184–186]. For instance, a water-stable Tb-MOFbased fluorescent detection system was employed to sense DPA [187]. The fluorescence sensing was carried out with good sensitivity (e.g., via a direct sensor-analyte interaction without pretreatment). In another work, a colorimetric bimetallic Tb/Eu-MOF ratiometric sensor was constructed to sense Bacillus anthracis - through the identification of its biomarker (DPA) molecule in human serum samples [188]. The sensor was built to detect changes in the emission color (e.g., from red-orange to yellow) of Tb/Eu(BTC). Recently, a Eu-BTC MOF/rhodamine-derived dye nanocomposite was reported for the optical detection of DPA [188]. This sensor was employed to enhance the absorption/emission of a rhodamine dye in the presence of DPA with a colorimetric change observable by the naked eye. However, a reduction of the emission intensity was observed from the Eu-BTC MOF composite due to electron transfer from Eu-BTC to DPA. Thus, a colorimetric/ ratiometric fluorescent sensor was built for DPA detection based on the dye absorption spectra and fluorescence quenching of EuBTC MOF. The developed sensor showed a linear response $(50-$ $700~\mathrm{nM},$ and high selectivity with LOD value of $0.52~\upmu\mathrm{M}.$ . More importantly, the Eu-BTC MOF/rhodamine-based sensor was found to exhibit good selectivity towards DPA in the presence of some interferents such as glycine (Gly), benzoic acid (BA), D-aspartic (Asp), and methionine (Met).
Further, thin films of RE-MOFs have been reported for the colorimetric sensing of pharmaceutical products [189]. These authors developed mixed crystal thin films of $\mathtt{E u}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathtt{T b}_{1-\mathrm{x}}$ -BTC on ITO glass for the detection of various pharmaceutical molecules (e.g., caffeine, clotetracycline, and sulfachinoxalin). The varying host–guest interactions allowed the detection of different analytes depending on the color observed by the naked eye (Fig. 15). Two MOF films $\mathrm{\small{[Tb_{2}(A I P)_{2}(H_{2}O)_{10}]\cdot(A I P)\cdot4H_{2}O}}$ (Tb-AIP, $\mathsf{A I P}=5$ -Aminobenzene-1,3 -dicarboxylic acid) and $\mathrm{\Delta[Eu_{2}(B C A)_{3}(H_{2}O)(D M F)_{3}]{\cdot0.5\mathrm{DMF}{\cdot}H_{2}O}}$ (Eu-BCA, $\mathsf{B C A}\ =\ 2{,}2^{\prime}$ -biquinoline- $^{\cdot4,4^{\prime}}$ -dicarboxylate) were also reported for the measurement of nitrofuran antibiotics (NFA) [190,191]. The Eu-BCA films were coated on a stainless steel wire mesh (SSWM) based on a $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nano-anchor fixation method. The developed films exhibited the characteristic red emission of ${\sf E u}^{3+}$ when excited by UV light. The emission was quenched specifically by nitrofurazone (NFZ) and nitrofuran antibiotics (e.g., nitrofurantoin (NFT), with LOD of 0.21 and $0.16~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ , respectively [190]. Similarly, Tb-AIP films were constructed by binding poly(methyl methacrylate) polymer with MOF fillers. The developed MOFbased films showed four characteristic transitions of the $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ ions in its emission spectrum upon excitation at $360~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . These films were thus employed as platforms for the sensing of NFAs. In this case, the luminescent spectra of Tb-AIPs films were efficiently quenched by NFZ and NFT due to the strong inner filter-effect existing between them. The Tb-AIP films demonstrated sensitive and selective sensing of NFZ and NFT (LOD of 0.35 and $0.30~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ , respectively) [191].
The application of a heterometallic RE-MOF $(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}/\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}.$ - $\mathrm{Zr_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{4}(O_{2}C-C_{6}H_{2}-C O_{2}(C O_{2}H)_{2})_{6}\cdot\cdot\mathrm{{}}M_{2}O)}$ has been documented towards the development of a turn-on type diagnosis platform for vinyl chloride carcinogen (VCM) [192]. The developed MOF probe was utilized for the selective and sensitive detection of thiodiglycolic acid (TDGA), which is known as a major metabolite of VCM in human urine (e.g., $\mathrm{{LOD}}=89\ \mathrm{\ng}{\cdot}\mathrm{{mL}}^{-1}$ and response time $=4~\mathrm{{min}}$ ). These authors also translated the technique into a portable dipstick device to measure VCM in real urine samples. RE-MOFs have also been explored for the sensing of viral RNA, which is important for disease detection in the early stages. Interestingly, a water-stable dysprosium-based MOF (Dys-MOF) composed of zwitterionic carboxylate was employed in a fluorescence assay for the detection of Ebolavirus RNA $(\mathrm{LOD}=160~\mathrm{pM},$ [193]. The structural properties of the Dys-MOF enabled strong electrostatic, hydrogen bonding, and $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking interactions with the fluorophore-labeled single-stranded probe DNA (FAM-probe DNA). Fluorescence quenching characterized the sensor during the formation of FAM-probe DNA. However, the fluorescence was restored in the presence of the target RNA sequence because of a higher affinity between the probe-DNA and the target RNA sequence. The results demonstrate the potential applications of metal–zwitterionic carboxylate compounds towards early Ebolavirus disease diagnosis.
The utility of RE-MOFs for electrochemical sensing applications has not yet been explored sufficiently mainly due to their low electro-activity, owing to poor transfer/hopping of electrons through metal-linker bonds. Therefore, some efforts have been made to increase the conductivity of RE-MOFs using various strategies like the selection of suitable metal–ligand combinations, incorporation of semiconducting guest molecules, and composite/ hybrid formation. For example, a Ce-MOF-based impedimetric sensor was employed as an aptasensor of ATP molecules [194]. For the construction of the sensor, bare gold electrodes were modified with Ce-MOF using electrostatic and covalent interactions. Next, ATP specific aptamers were conjugated with Ce-MOF by hydrogen bonding, $\pi\cdot\pi$ stacking, and electrostatic interactions. The specific binding of ATP and its aptamer caused conformational changes in the aptamer strands. This led to changes in the electrochemical impedance of the developed probes, as measured by electrochemical impedance spectroscopy. The obtained sensor with good sensitivity $(5.6~\mathrm{nM})$ was applied efficiently for the sensing of ATP in many types of real samples (serum of cancer patients) to demonstrate its potential in clinical diagnosis.
## 4.3.2. Sensing of temperature
The accurate measurement of temperature is desirable in many situations, including in physiological and pathological fields. RE-MOFs have enabled the development of rapid, precise, and non-invasive temperature sensing systems (Table 9) with good sensitivity [195–197]. Ratiometric thermometers made up of mixed lanthanides $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ $(545~\mathrm{nm})$ and ${\mathsf{E u}}^{3+}$ $(613\ \mathrm{nm})$ containing ligands with high triplet excited energy states have been reported. For example, an RE-MOF $\mathrm{'Eu}_{0.0069}\mathrm{Tb}_{0.9931}$ -dmbc; where dmbc $=2$ , 5-dimethoxy-1, 4-benzenedicarboxylate)-based thermometer was reported for the instant detection of temperature ranging from 10 to $300\mathrm{~K~}$ [197]. The same group later used another ligand 5-(4-carboxyphenyl)-2,6-pyridinedicarboxylic acid $(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{cpda})$ with $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ and ${\sf E u}^{3+}$ for the development of a mixed lanthanide MOF (Tb0.957Eu0.043cpda)-based ratiometric/colorimetric luminescent thermometer (temperature range 40–300 K) [198]. In a similar study, 5-(pyridin-4-yl)isophthalate (PIA) linker was used for the development of a mixed lanthanide MOF $(\mathrm{Tb}_{0.9}\mathrm{Eu}0_{0.1}–\mathrm{PIA})$ 1 thermometer with very high sensitivity $(3.53\%{\sf K}^{-1}\mathrm{~,~}$ ) [199].
As another strategy for the development of a ratiometric thermometer, luminescent perylene dye molecules were encapsulated inside the pores of an Eu-MOF [196]. The MOF-dye-based thermometer exhibited a temperature-dependent change of the luminescence intensity over the physiological range of temperature with a sensitivity of $1.28\%~\%$ at $20\ ^{\circ}{\mathsf C}.$ . A temperaturedependent color change-based thermometer has been reported using mixed RE-MOFs. In this example, three lanthanides $(\mathrm{Ln~\Omega~}=\mathrm{~\Eu~}$ , Gd, and Tb) were coordinated with a DSB (3,5- disulfobenzoate) ligand for the construction of an isostructural RE-MOF $[\{\mathrm{Ln}_{7}(3,5\mathrm{-DSB})_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{9}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{15}\}.4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}]$ . The multimetal combination of Eu (and/or Tb) with Gd in the RE-MOF structure and triplet state emission from the DSB ligand contributed to temperature-dependent (10–300 K) alteration of the color of the
MOF. This change was observed over the entire visible range for a wide temperature range. The sensitivity of the system was reported to be $32\%\mathrm{K}^{-1}$ [195]. Likewise, hybrid thin films of two RE-MOFs [ $\mathsf{M O F1}=\mathsf{Z n}_{8}(\mathsf{A d})_{4}(\mathsf{B P D C})_{6}\mathsf{O}\cdot2\mathsf{M e}_{2}\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ and ${\sf M O F}2={\sf R E}({\bf B}$ PDC)(Adenine); where $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B P D C}=2$ , $2^{\prime}$ bipyridine-6,60 dicarboxylic acid] were reported for temperature sensing. Thin films of the above materials were prepared in a medium of polymerized ethyl methacrylate (EMA) and 4-vinylpyridine (VPD). Depending on the ratio of Ln ions used for MOF formation, the hybrid polymer thin films displayed different colors, such as blue, red, and blue-green. Particularly, the Tb0.999 Eu0.001-MOF hybrid was an efficient luminescent thermometer which changed its color (e.g., from bluegreen to pink) as the temperature was varied from 100 to $320~\mathrm{K}.$ This property was accounted for by the energy transfer from $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ $\rightarrow\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ [200].
Similarly, a $\mathrm{\Eu/Tb}$ co-doped MOF $(\mathrm{Eu}_{0.0878}\mathrm{Tb}_{0.9122}\mathrm{L})$ was prepared by using dicarboxylic acid, 5-(4-(tetrazol-5-yl)phenyl) isophthalic acid $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}\right)$ as the organic linker. The synthesized material was reported to be a ratiometric luminescent thermometer, which exhibited a linear relationship from 75 to $250~\mathrm{K}$ using the emission intensity ratio of $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ $\cdot544~\mathrm{nm})$ to ${\mathsf{E u}}^{3+}$ ( $\mathrm{614~nm}\cdot$ [201]. Other than $\mathrm{Tb/Eu}$ ions, some other lanthanides (such as $\mathsf{N d}/\mathsf{Y b}$ ions) have also been employed for the construction of MOFbased luminescent thermometers. The Nd/Yb co-doped MOF $(\mathrm{Nd}_{0.577}\mathrm{Yb}_{0.423}\mathrm{BDC-F}_{4})$ showed NIR luminescence for the sensitive quantitation of temperature in the physiological range (293– 313 K) [202]. Recently, ratiometric dual-color emitting RE-based MOF hybrids $\mathrm{[Tb_{1-x}E u_{x}L}$ $\mathbf{\check{X}}=0.05$ , 0.1 and 0.2)] were reported as temperature sensing materials. $\mathrm{Tb}_{0.9}\mathrm{Eu}_{0.1}\mathrm{L}$ was reported to be a highly stable and biocompatible probe with temperature sensing ability from 303 to $423\ \mathrm{K}.$ The sensitivity of the above probe was $1.75\%\mathrm{K}^{-1}$ at $423\ \mathrm{K}$ making it useful in biomedical systems [203].
## 4.3.3. Sensing of pH
The pH-dependent photoluminescence of RE ion-based MOFs is a highly useful property for their application as pH sensors (Table 9) [204]. The PSM of MOF-253 with TTA (2- thenoyltrifluoroacetone) led to the realization of two types of ${\sf E u}^{3+}$ (Eu1 and Eu2) in MOFs. The Eu1 and Eu2 possessed different excitation wavelengths ( $330~\mathrm{{nm}}$ and $375~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , respectively) due to different coordination environments [204]. The Eu1 ions were linked to only the bipyridine ligand, while the Eu2 ions were bound to bipyridine as well as TTA. The emission intensity due to Eu2 ions was found to be sensitive to pH and decreased linearly in the pH range of 5–7.2 while the fluorescence of Eu1 ions remained unaltered. In the presence of $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}^{+}$ protons, the six-membered chelate ring of the ${\sf E u}^{3+}$ -TTA complex gets disrupted, leading to a decrease of the MOF sensitization process (from $\upbeta$ -diketonate to ${\mathsf{E u}}^{3+}$ ) [205]. The pH was determined from the change of the ${}^{5}\mathrm{D}_{0}-{}^{7}\mathrm{F}_{2}$ emission intensity ratio of ${\sf E u}^{3+}$ ions (Eu1/Eu2) at an excitation wavelength of $375\mathrm{nm}$ . Further, a prototype $\mathsf{p H}$ sensor (range 5–7.5) was developed using ITQMOF-3 ( $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ -based MOF) containing two different ${\sf E u}^{3+}$ sites (Eu-1 and Eu-2) and $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ PhenDCA ligand (L) [206]. The emission spectrum of the synthesized RE-MOF confirmed the presence of two different ${\sf E u}^{3+}$ ions due to the presence of two nondegenerate ${^5}\mathrm{D}0-{^7}\mathrm{F}0$ transition lines (e.g., at 579.0 (Eu-2) and 580.7 (Eu-1) nm). Accordingly, the RE-MOF crystals in contact with analyte solutions of increasing pH were characterized by an increased fluorescent intensity of the Eu2 line, while there was no change in the Eu1 line. Thus, the intensity ratio (Ir) of the two ${\sf E u}^{3+}$ lines could be used for the determination of pH in a solution. The sensing strategy was further adopted for the design of a miniaturized pH sensor prototype, which used a commercial optic fiber with a diameter of $1.5\mathrm{mm}$ for intracellular pH measurement [206]. As another example, a $\mathtt{E u/T b}$ co-doped MOF $\scriptstyle\left[\operatorname{Ln}_{2}(\operatorname{D-cam})\right.$ $\left(\operatorname{Himdc}\right)_{2}$ $(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})_{2}]$ $\langle\mathrm{Ln}=\mathrm{Eu}\ 1$ , Tb 2, where $\mathsf{D-H}_{2}\mathsf{c a m}=(1R,3S)\mathsf{-}1,2,2^{\prime}$ -trimethylcyclopentane-1,3-dicarboxylic acid and $\mathrm{H_{3}i m d c}~=~1\mathrm{H}-$ Imidazole-4,5-dicarboxylic acid) was prepared by a hydrothermal method. This MOF showed a pH-dependent change of luminescence in the pH range of 6.8–8.0. The Tb/Eu co-doped MOF (ratio of 0.7:0.3) also exhibited temperature sensing properties over 100 to $450~\mathrm{K}$ [207]. Similarly, highly porous Tb-MOF synthesized with isophthalate $(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}^{+}\mathrm{Cl}^{-})$ as an organic linker and embedded with viologen species was used as the fluorophore. The resulting tetranuclear crystals of the MOF possessed multiple photofunctionality features such as high photoluminescence and photochromism, which made it useful as a potential pH sensor. The luminescence intensity of Tb-MOF linearly decreased under acidic conditions from $\mathsf{p H}2$ to 7 [208]. Tb-MOF nanoparticles, synthesized by linking $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ ions with 1,10 phenanthroline in the presence of DMF, was applied as a pH sensor based on intramolecular charge transfer between the electron donor and electron acceptor parts present in DMF. DMF showed an antenna effect for $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ luminescence. The protonation of $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}^{+}$ would result in charge transfer in DMF accompanied by the antenna effect for $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ . The system could sense the pH value in extremely acidic solutions, such as gastric juice [209].
## 4.3.4. Sensing of miscellaneous analytes
RE-MOFs have been utilized for various sensing applications (e.g., $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{2}$ , ascorbic acid, and many other small molecules, Table 10) [210–213]. For instance, lanthanum and graphene oxide-based nanocomposites (LaMOF-GO) were prepared to efficiently isolate hemoglobin (Hb) protein from whole blood. The incorporation of graphene oxide (GO) into the MOF [La(BTC) $(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})_{6}]$ network changed its morphology (e.g., from rectangular rods to irregular thick blocks). It also increased its surface area (e.g., from 14.8 to $26.6~\mathrm{cm}^{2}/\mathrm{g})$ , without altering its structure. The LaMOF-GO nanocomposite exhibited superior adsorption properties towards proteins because of the high $\pi{-}\pi$ hydrophobic interactions between the Hb protein and GO. LaMOF-GO was an excellent adsorbent for Hb with $63\%$ recovery when applied in $4\mathrm{mM}$ BR buffer with $0.5~\mathrm{mol}/\mathrm{l}$ NaCl [212].
Note that ascorbic acid (AA: vitamin C) plays multiple roles in biochemical processes in our body. The development of sensors to detect ascorbic acid is quite meaningful in the diagnosis of heart diseases and cancer. A highly luminescent cerium-based MOF (ZJU 136-Ce) with TPTC (1,1,4,1- terphenyl-2,4,4,5- tetracarboxylic acid) as the organic linker was used for the turn-on fluorescent detection of AA. The Ce-MOF showed a highly stable crystal structure by forming a 3-D porous network. The change of the luminescence during the sensing process was ascribed to the redox reaction (e.g., between AA and $\mathsf{C e}^{4+^{.}}$ ). An LOD as low as $7~\mathrm{{nM}}$ for AA was achievable [211]. Later, a novel ${\tt E u}^{3+}$ -based MOF $[\mathrm{\{(H_{3}O}}$ $[\mathrm{Eu(SBDB)(H_{2}O)_{2}}]\}_{\mathrm{n}}]$ was synthesized by a solvothermal process using $\mathsf{H}_{4}S\mathsf{B}\mathrm{T}\mathsf{B}$ (5-sulfo-benzene-1,2,4-tricarboxylic acid) as the organic linker. The MOF complex displayed a supernetwork structure with high chemical and thermal stabilities. The luminescent MOF was employed as an optical sensor for highly sensitive AA detection in aqueous solutions based on fluorescence quenching. It could detect up to $5~{\upmu\mathrm{M}}$ AA in the range up to $10^{-4}\mathrm{M}$ [214].
Note that hippuric acid (HA) is one of the unique biological markers for toluene exposure in humans [215]. It has been identified as uremic toxin associated with phenylketonuria, tyrosinemia, and other inborn errors of metabolism. For example, a ${\sf E u}^{3+}$ -functionalized RE-MOF synthesized using $\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{BTEC}$ (benzene1,2,4,5- tetracarboxylic acid) as the organic ligand has been proposed to build a fluorescent probe for selective sensing of HA in urine samples [216]. The luminescent intensity of HAincorporated $\mathtt{E u}^{3+}@1$ (at $614~\mathrm{{nm}}$ ) was quenched compared to that of $\mathtt{E u}^{3\mathrm{+}}@1$ alone. The analysis of HA demonstrated about a $80\%$ reduction of the luminescence intensity of $\mathtt{E u}^{3+}@1$ at $614~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . Moreover, the emission lifetime analysis of ${\sf E u}^{3+}$ showed that there was a decrease in the lifetime of ${\sf E u}^{3+}$ from 176 to $76\upmu s$ only in the presence of HA. The fluorescent intensity ratio exhibited a strong correlation with the HA concentration to yield a detection limit of $9~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}/{\mathrm{ml}}$ across the detection range of $0.05{-}8~\mathrm{mg/ml}$ . The reason for HA-induced luminescence quenching can be accounted for by two reasons. The first one is the weak coordination of HA to ${\sf E u}^{3+}$ sites, which increases non-radiative deactivations to reduce the luminescence [217,218]. Secondly, the intermolecular interactions (such as H-bond and $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking) between ligands and HA led to intersystem crossing to suppress the energy transfer to ${\sf E u}^{3+}$ ions [219]. The results indicated that the MOF probe could detect HA with a fast response time $({\sim}1\ \mathrm{min})$ , along with high recyclability and excellent selectivity [216].
Lysophospathidic acid (LPA) is a biological marker, which plays a crucial role in the pathophysiology of ovarian cancer [220]. The detection of LPA can help in the diagnosis of ovarian carcinogenesis during early periods of onset. In a study, two different lanthanide MOFs were synthesized using $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B P D C}$ (2,2 bipyridine-6,6 dicarboxylic acid) as the linker molecules and $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ and ${\sf E u}^{3+}$ metalcentered ions. Three mixed-crystal MOFs $(\mathrm{Eu_{x}T b_{1-x}}–Z\mathrm{MOF})$ were also prepared by varying the stoichiometry of ${\mathrm{Tb}}^{3+}{\mathrm{:Eu}}^{3+}$ . These zeolite-like structured MOFs (Tb-ZMOF and Eu-ZMOF) exhibited significant changes in their characteristic emission band intensities at $546\ \mathrm{nm}$ and $612\ \mathrm{nm}$ , respectively, upon the addition of LPA [221,222]. The luminescence intensity of Tb-ZMOF was quenched (at $546~\mathrm{nm}^{\cdot}$ ) in the presence of LPA while the luminescence intensity of Eu-ZMOF was enhanced (at $612\mathrm{nm}\cdot$ ) upon LPA addition. The intensity ratio (integrated intensity, $\mathrm{I}=\mathrm{I_{Eu}}/\mathrm{I_{Tb}})$ of the mixed-crystal MOFs $(\mathrm{Eu_{x}T b_{1-x}}–Z\mathrm{MOF})$ also showed a similar trend towards LPA as Tb-ZMOF and Eu-ZMOF (e.g., decrease $(\mathrm{Tb}^{2+})$ or increase $(\mathsf{E u}^{2+})$ of the intensity of characteristic emission band). The as-synthesized MOFs were able to detect trace quantities of LPA in blood plasma within the range of $1.4\ \mathrm{~-~}\ 43.3\ \upmu\mathrm{M}$ In particular, the mixed ZMOF-3 showed selective detection of LPA with good sensitivity. The sensing mechanism relies on the energy transfer process in which the intensity of ${\sf E u}^{3+}$ increased (at $613~\mathrm{nm}\cdot$ while that of $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ transitions (at $546~\mathrm{nm}$ ) decreased in the presence of LPA, as demonstrated by fluorescence lifetime measurements of mixed ZMOF-3 [221].
Interestingly, a $\mathsf{E u}^{3+}$ -loaded Sc-MOF was used as the highly selective fluorescent probe for smartphone assisted detection of
PGA in human serum and urine. The RE-MOF sensing system demonstrated astonishing characteristics such as faster detection $(>5~s)$ , a long lifetime, and high sensitivity. The mechanism of PGA detection was based on the ligand–metal charge transfer in which –COOH groups present in MOF could sense ${\sf E u}^{3+}$ fluorescence with enhanced emission of $\operatorname{Eu@MOFs}$ in the presence of PGA. It could detect PGA in a broad linear range $\cdot0.02\mathrm{-}0.05~\mathrm{mg/}$ ml) with a LOD of 4.16 ppb [223]. Similarly, a nano-RE-MOF composed of $\mathsf{C e}^{3+}$ CPNs (coordination polymer nanoparticles) and ATP molecules as the linking agent in Tris-HCl buffer was employed for the development of a selective sensor for $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ detection [210]. The detection of hydrogen peroxide $\left(\operatorname{H}_{2}0_{2}\right)$ as one of the principal agents of ROS plays a significant role in biocellular machinery. The sensing strategy was based on the oxidation of ATP- $.\mathsf{C e}^{3+}$ -Tris to nonfluorescent ATP- ${\mathsf{C e}}^{4+}$ -Tris upon the addition of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . The CPNs displayed a low detection limit of $0.6\ \mathrm{nM}$ in the range of $1\mathrm{\nM}$ to $80~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ . Moreover, the same nanoprobe was employed for selective detection of glucose with a detection limit of $65~\mathrm{{nM}}$ in the range of $0.1\mathrm{-}100\upmu\mathrm{M}$ [210]. A pH sensitive Eu-MOF was built using ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf N D C}$ (Naphthalene-2,6-dicarboxylic acid) linker coordinated to ${\sf E u}^{3+}$ metal ions [213]. The MOF showed dual emission behavior by switching the luminescence between red and blue color influenced by $\mathsf{p H}$ in the range from 3 to 4. Therefore, it could also be used as a pH-induced fluorescent sensor. The MOF was able to selectively detect Asp and Glu (acidic amino acids) in aqueous solutions. The LODs for Asp and Glu were found to be 21 and $22.4~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ , respectively [213].
## 5. Summary and future outlook
It is recognized that the synthesis and application of luminescent RE-MOFs is a growing area of MOF research. Hence, an effort was made to review the latest progress achieved in the controlled synthesis of luminescent RE-MOFs and their applications in various biomedical fields, especially optical imaging, MRI-guided photodynamic therapy, drug delivery, and biosensing with tailored responses to targeted biomolecular cells (e.g., organs, tissues, or enzymes). From the perspective of materials science, the advanced properties of RE-MOFs (e.g., architectural versatility, shape/size, porosity, surface functionality, internal cages, and electronic properties) can expand their applications in diverse biomedical fields because of associated advantages, including (i) high fluorescence/ optical intensity, (ii) high loading capacity for drugs, (iii) excellent biocompatibility, and (iv) biological cell targeting. These merits can be precisely tuned through the deliberate choice of MOF building blocks (fluorescent organic ligands and RE ions). In fact, the magnetism and photoluminescence of RE-MOFs are strongly dependent on the coordination chemistry between RE metal centers and organic ligands. Therefore, the selection of a suitable selfassembly strategy is critical to enhance or maintain the fluorescence/emission properties of luminescent RE-MOFs used for biosensing/bioimaging applications. This is because such selection may be important to minimize the quenching effect of axially coordinated solvent molecules (e.g., water) or other quencher molecules (e.g., $C{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{H}$ and –OH). It is also desirable to build RE-MOFs with modulated energy transfer mechanisms and a high optical quantum yields, particularly in the NIR region, for practical biosensing and dual T1-T2 MRI contrast imaging. This could be achieved by using biological chromophoric ligands (e.g., porphyrin) or mixed ligands to improve the luminescent features of RE-MOFs due to the antenna effect for practical biosensing/bioimaging. Thus, it is important to stress that the chosen chromophoric or mixed ligands should not disrupt the magnetic coupling between the RE metal centers in RE-MOFs. As such, RE-MOFs constructed with such functionality can offer a new route to develop multifunctional platforms for different biomedical fields.
RE-MOFs have also been demonstrated to be effective platforms in biosensing and drug delivery applications. Various RE-MOFbased sensors have been reported for the detection of several analytes such as antibiotics, vitamins, metal ions, and disease biomarkers as well as pH and temperature. Also, the optical properties of various RE-MOFs have been utilized in dual applications, including optical imaging and disease diagnostics (like CT-scan and MRI). These advantageous properties of RE-MOFs allow expanding their applicabilities toward the successful detection and treatment of cell tumors in different biological fluids. However, the practical applicability prospect of RE-MOFs in biomedical fields still remains in its infant stage. This is due to several hindering factors: (i) high synthesis cost, (ii) low yield of RE-MOFs on the lab-scale, (iii) poor reproducibility of materials, (iv) unknown chemical stability of RE-MOFs in biological cells, and (v) high toxicity of free RE ions.
Therefore, in the future, considerable attention should be paid to improve cellular responses, biocompatibility, and the thermodynamic/kinetic inertness of RE-MOFs in human cells. In addition, efforts should be made to develop eco-friendly and economic methodologies for the high-yield synthesis of multifunctional REMOF materials. In addition, more in-depth studies are required to address the lifetime and long-term biocompatibility/biotoxicity of RE-MOFs in biological cells for their practical expansion in biomedical fields. Hence, the controlled growth of RE-MOFs via tailored coordination of RE (or lanthanide) ions with organic ligands is expected to pave a new avenue to develop fascinating multifunctional regimes in biomedical applications.
